window.EUROPA_EN_CONTENT = {"cities":[{"id":"rome","name":"Rome","overview":"Rome on the Tiber served in turn as the political heart of the Republic and Empire, a major sacred city of Latin Christianity, and Italy's capital. Ancient public buildings, papal projects and modern state institutions stand side by side, tracing the city's shift from imperial rule to religious authority and then to the nation-state.","milestones":[{"year":-27,"date":"27 BCE","title":"Augustus and the Principate","text":"The Senate granted Octavian the title Augustus. He preserved the outward forms of republican government while exercising real power through the army and a combination of offices.","polity":"Roman Empire","sourceIds":["rome-augustus"]},{"year":800,"date":"25 December 800","title":"Charlemagne crowned in Rome","text":"Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne emperor, renewing Rome's role as a stage for relations between imperial and papal power in western Europe.","polity":"Papal States / Carolingian Empire","sourceIds":["rome-charlemagne"]},{"year":1870,"date":"1870","title":"Annexed to the Kingdom of Italy","text":"Rome was annexed to the Kingdom of Italy, ending papal temporal rule over the city and marking a new stage in Italian unification.","polity":"Kingdom of Italy","sourceIds":["rome-capital"]},{"year":1871,"date":"1871","title":"Rome becomes Italy's capital","text":"Rome was formally established as the capital, and national institutions began moving there. Palazzo Madama became the seat of the kingdom's Senate.","polity":"Kingdom of Italy","sourceIds":["rome-capital"]}],"sources":[{"id":"rome-augustus","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art: Colossal Head of Augustus and the Principate","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/251114","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","supports":"27 BCE — Augustus and the Principate"},{"id":"rome-charlemagne","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art: France, 500–1000 CE","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/de/toah/ht/06/euwf","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","supports":"25 December 800 — Charlemagne crowned in Rome"},{"id":"rome-capital","title":"Italian Senate: The Senate of the Kingdom and Rome as Capital","url":"https://www.senato.it/en/parliamentary-business/history-palazzo-madama/senate-kingdom","institution":"Senate of the Italian Republic","supports":"1870 — Annexed to the Kingdom of Italy; 1871 — Rome becomes Italy's capital"}]},{"id":"constantinople","name":"Constantinople / Istanbul","overview":"Growing from the ancient Greek colony of Byzantium, this city commanding the Bosporus became a lasting center of Eastern Roman imperial power, religion and trade. Its court, harbors and Hagia Sophia gave it an international stature. Crusader occupation and Ottoman conquest changed its rulers without ending urban life.","milestones":[{"year":330,"date":"330","title":"Constantine's new capital","text":"Constantine I established the rebuilt city of Byzantium as a new imperial capital, naming it Constantinople after himself and strengthening the Roman Empire's political center in the east.","polity":"Roman Empire","sourceIds":["constantinople-byzantium"]},{"year":537,"date":"27 December 537","title":"Hagia Sophia dedicated","text":"The cathedral rebuilt under Justinian I was completed. Its vast dome and spacious interior expressed the empire's religious and architectural ambitions.","polity":"Eastern Roman Empire","sourceIds":["constantinople-sophia"]},{"year":1204,"date":"1204","title":"The Fourth Crusade captures the city","text":"Crusaders captured and sacked Constantinople, then established the Latin Empire. Eastern Roman political and cultural centers shifted to Nicaea and other cities.","polity":"Eastern Roman Empire / Latin Empire","sourceIds":["constantinople-byzantium"]},{"year":1261,"date":"1261","title":"Eastern Roman rule restored","text":"Forces from Nicaea recaptured Constantinople. Michael VIII restored the Eastern Roman court, beginning the Palaiologan dynasty's rule in the city.","polity":"Eastern Roman Empire","sourceIds":["constantinople-byzantium"]},{"year":1453,"date":"29 May 1453","title":"Ottoman conquest of Constantinople","text":"Mehmed II's forces entered the city, ending Eastern Roman rule centered on Constantinople. The city subsequently became the Ottoman capital.","polity":"Ottoman Empire","sourceIds":["constantinople-fall"]}],"sources":[{"id":"constantinople-byzantium","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art: Byzantium, c. 330–1453","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/byzantium-ca-330-1453","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","supports":"330 — Constantine's new capital; 1204 — The Fourth Crusade captures the city; 1261 — Eastern Roman rule restored"},{"id":"constantinople-sophia","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art: Hagia Sophia, 532–537","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/hagia-sophia-532-37","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","supports":"27 December 537 — Hagia Sophia dedicated"},{"id":"constantinople-fall","title":"British Library: The Last Day of Constantinople","url":"https://www.bl.uk/stories/blogs/posts/the-last-day-of-constantinople","institution":"British Library","supports":"29 May 1453 — Ottoman conquest of Constantinople"}]},{"id":"paris","name":"Paris","overview":"Paris grew along the Seine from ancient Lutetia into a city of royal power, universities and commerce. Revolutions turned its neighborhoods and squares into arenas of political participation. Nineteenth-century reconstruction shaped its boulevards and streets, while occupation and liberation during the Second World War left enduring memories.","milestones":[{"year":1789,"date":"14 July 1789","title":"Storming of the Bastille","text":"Parisians stormed the Bastille, making popular mobilization in the city a major force in the French Revolution. New municipal institutions and the National Guard followed.","polity":"Kingdom of France","sourceIds":["paris-carnavalet"]},{"year":1853,"date":"1853","title":"Haussmann's transformation of Paris","text":"Napoleon III appointed Georges-Eugène Haussmann prefect of the Seine, launching a sweeping reorganization of roads, public infrastructure and urban space.","polity":"Second French Empire","sourceIds":["paris-carnavalet"]},{"year":1871,"date":"March–May 1871","title":"The Paris Commune","text":"Political conflict after military defeat and the siege gave rise to the Paris Commune. Government forces from Versailles retook the city in May, with heavy casualties during the repression.","polity":"Paris Commune / French Third Republic","sourceIds":["paris-commune"]},{"year":1944,"date":"25 August 1944","title":"Liberation of Paris","text":"Following a Resistance uprising, the French 2nd Armored Division and US forces entered Paris. The German garrison surrendered, ending the occupation of the capital.","polity":"Provisional Government of the French Republic","sourceIds":["paris-liberation"]}],"sources":[{"id":"paris-carnavalet","title":"Musée Carnavalet: The City History Galleries","url":"https://www.carnavalet.paris.fr/en/le-nouveau-parcours","institution":"Musée Carnavalet – History of Paris","supports":"14 July 1789 — Storming of the Bastille; 1853 — Haussmann's transformation of Paris"},{"id":"paris-commune","title":"Paris Archives: The Paris Commune","url":"https://archives.paris.fr/offre-pedagogique/ressources-pedagogiques/la-commune-de-paris","institution":"Paris Archives","supports":"March–May 1871 — The Paris Commune"},{"id":"paris-liberation","title":"Museum of the Liberation of Paris: The Liberation of Paris","url":"https://www.museeliberation-leclerc-moulin.paris.fr/ressources/ressource-pour-les-scolaires/eclairage-sur-une-oeuvre-la-liberation-de-paris","institution":"Museum of the Liberation of Paris","supports":"25 August 1944 — Liberation of Paris"}]},{"id":"london","name":"London","overview":"London began as Roman Londinium near a Thames crossing, where the river linked the hinterland, the port and overseas trade. It grew into a major political and commercial center of England and later Britain. Fire and air raids devastated neighborhoods, while successive rebuilding left layers of urban history spanning antiquity and the modern era.","milestones":[{"year":50,"date":"c. 47–50 CE","title":"The rise of Londinium","text":"The Romans established Londinium on the north bank of the Thames. Bridges, roads and river transport helped it grow rapidly into a major commercial center in Britain.","polity":"Roman Empire","sourceIds":["london-roman"]},{"year":1666,"date":"2–6 September 1666","title":"The Great Fire of London","text":"A fire spreading from a bakery in Pudding Lane destroyed much of the City of London and Old St Paul's Cathedral. Rebuilding largely retained the existing street pattern.","polity":"Kingdom of England","sourceIds":["london-fire"]},{"year":1940,"date":"From September 1940","title":"The London Blitz","text":"From 7 September, Nazi Germany launched sustained mass air raids on London. Docks, homes and public buildings were damaged as civilian life centered on air-raid precautions and rescue work.","polity":"United Kingdom","sourceIds":["london-blitz"]}],"sources":[{"id":"london-roman","title":"London Museum: A Timeline of Roman London","url":"https://www.londonmuseum.org.uk/collections/london-stories/timeline-roman-london-londinium/","institution":"London Museum","supports":"c. 47–50 CE — The rise of Londinium"},{"id":"london-fire","title":"London Museum: The Great Fire of London","url":"https://www.londonmuseum.org.uk/collections/london-stories/great-fire-of-london/","institution":"London Museum","supports":"2–6 September 1666 — The Great Fire of London"},{"id":"london-blitz","title":"London Museum: London's Blitz—A City at War","url":"https://www.londonmuseum.org.uk/collections/london-stories/londons-blitz-a-city-at-war/","institution":"London Museum","supports":"From September 1940 — The London Blitz"}]},{"id":"aachen","name":"Aachen","overview":"Aachen's hot springs were used in Roman times, and Charlemagne developed the town into a major royal residence. Its palace chapel, scholars and coronations gave this small city exceptional political influence. Later kings invoked Charlemagne's memory to legitimize their rule, while modern Aachen connected this heritage with European cooperation.","milestones":[{"year":800,"date":"c. 800","title":"A center of the Carolingian court","text":"Charlemagne built a palace and chapel in Aachen and made it his main residence. Court scholars helped reform education, writing and religious culture.","polity":"Carolingian Empire","sourceIds":["aachen-city","aachen-charlemagne"]},{"year":814,"date":"28 January 814","title":"Charlemagne's death and burial","text":"Charlemagne died in Aachen and was buried in St Mary's Church. His tomb and the later veneration of his relics made the city a major center of his commemoration.","polity":"Carolingian Empire","sourceIds":["aachen-charlemagne"]},{"year":936,"date":"936","title":"Coronation of Otto I","text":"Otto I was crowned king in Aachen, reinforcing the city's importance as a ceremonial center of German kingship.","polity":"East Frankish Kingdom","sourceIds":["aachen-charlemagne"]},{"year":1950,"date":"1950","title":"The first Charlemagne Prize","text":"Aachen awarded its first Charlemagne Prize, linking the city's historical memory to postwar efforts toward European cooperation and political and economic integration.","polity":"Federal Republic of Germany","sourceIds":["aachen-charlemagne"]}],"sources":[{"id":"aachen-charlemagne","title":"Aachen Cathedral: Charlemagne, His Court and the Coronation Tradition","url":"https://www.aachenerdom.de/en/a-place-of-history/charlemagne/","institution":"Aachen Cathedral","supports":"c. 800 — A center of the Carolingian court; 28 January 814 — Charlemagne's death and burial; 936 — Coronation of Otto I; 1950 — The first Charlemagne Prize"},{"id":"aachen-city","title":"City of Aachen: The Aachen Chronoscopes","url":"https://www.aachen.de/in-aachen-leben/kultur/geschichte/die-aachener-chronoskope/","institution":"City of Aachen","supports":"c. 800 — A center of the Carolingian court"}]},{"id":"vienna","name":"Vienna","overview":"Vienna grew from a Roman settlement on the Danube into a medieval city and an important Habsburg political center. Sieges, court culture, Baroque building and music shaped its character. The nineteenth-century Congress and twentieth-century State Treaty also made it a meeting place for reshaping Europe's political order.","milestones":[{"year":1529,"date":"1529","title":"The first Ottoman siege","text":"Suleiman I's army besieged Vienna but failed to take it. The city served as a major frontier stronghold in the contest between Habsburg and Ottoman power.","polity":"Habsburg Austria / Ottoman Empire","sourceIds":["vienna-history"]},{"year":1683,"date":"September 1683","title":"The second siege lifted","text":"Ottoman forces again besieged Vienna. A relief army from Poland and the states of the Holy Roman Empire won nearby, lifting the siege and changing the course of the war.","polity":"Habsburg Monarchy / Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth / Ottoman Empire","sourceIds":["vienna-history"]},{"year":1815,"date":"1814–1815","title":"The Congress of Vienna","text":"European representatives negotiated a political order after the Napoleonic Wars. The Final Act, signed on 9 June 1815, reorganized territories and international relations.","polity":"Austrian Empire","sourceIds":["vienna-congress"]},{"year":1955,"date":"15 May 1955","title":"The Austrian State Treaty signed","text":"Austria and the four occupying powers signed the State Treaty at the Belvedere Palace, laying the basis for full sovereignty and an end to the postwar occupation.","polity":"Republic of Austria","sourceIds":["vienna-austria"]}],"sources":[{"id":"vienna-history","title":"University of Oldenburg's Encyclopedia of Eastern European History: Vienna","url":"https://ome-lexikon.uni-oldenburg.de/en/places/vienna","institution":"University of Oldenburg, OME Encyclopedia","supports":"1529 — The first Ottoman siege; September 1683 — The second siege lifted"},{"id":"vienna-congress","title":"Austrian Commission for UNESCO: Final Document of the Congress of Vienna","url":"https://www.unesco.at/en/communication/documentary-heritage/memory-of-the-world-in-austria/final-document-of-the-congress-of-vienna-1815","institution":"Austrian Commission for UNESCO","supports":"1814–1815 — The Congress of Vienna"},{"id":"vienna-austria","title":"Austrian Foreign Ministry: History of Austria","url":"https://www.bmeia.gv.at/oev-wien/about-austria/history","institution":"Austrian Federal Ministry for European and International Affairs","supports":"15 May 1955 — The Austrian State Treaty signed"}]},{"id":"kyiv","name":"Kyiv","overview":"Kyiv stands on the Dnipro, where river and overland trade made it a major political and religious center of medieval Rus'. Its churches, monasteries and chronicle tradition reflect connections with the Byzantine world. After Mongol conquest and repeated changes of rule, the city became the capital of Soviet Ukraine and then independent Ukraine in the twentieth century.","milestones":[{"year":988,"date":"c. 988","title":"The ruler of Rus' adopts Christianity","text":"Vladimir adopted Christianity in the Byzantine tradition and forged a marriage alliance with the imperial family, transforming religious, architectural and cultural life in Kyiv and Rus'.","polity":"Rus'","sourceIds":["kyiv-baptism"]},{"year":1240,"date":"December 1240","title":"Mongol forces capture Kyiv","text":"Batu's Mongol army captured and sacked Kyiv. Heavy losses to its population and buildings further weakened the city's former political importance.","polity":"Mongol Empire (attacking power)","sourceIds":["kyiv-encyclopedia"]},{"year":1934,"date":"1934","title":"The Ukrainian Soviet capital moves to Kyiv","text":"The capital of the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic moved from Kharkiv to Kyiv, expanding the city's administrative role and industrial development.","polity":"Soviet Union / Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic","sourceIds":["kyiv-encyclopedia","kyiv-capital"]},{"year":1991,"date":"1991","title":"Capital of independent Ukraine","text":"After Ukraine declared independence, Kyiv changed from the administrative center of a Soviet republic into the political center of an independent state.","polity":"Ukraine","sourceIds":["kyiv-encyclopedia"]}],"sources":[{"id":"kyiv-baptism","title":"Smarthistory: Byzantium and Kievan Rus'","url":"https://smarthistory.org/byzantium-kievan-rus/","institution":"Smarthistory","supports":"c. 988 — The ruler of Rus' adopts Christianity"},{"id":"kyiv-encyclopedia","title":"Encyclopedia of Ukraine: Kyiv","url":"https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/pages%5CK%5CY%5CKyiv.htm","institution":"Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, Encyclopedia of Ukraine","supports":"December 1240 — Mongol forces capture Kyiv; 1934 — The Ukrainian Soviet capital moves to Kyiv; 1991 — Capital of independent Ukraine"},{"id":"kyiv-capital","title":"Vernadsky National Library of Ukraine: The 1934 Transfer of the Capital","url":"https://nbuv.gov.ua/node/4161","institution":"Vernadsky National Library of Ukraine","supports":"1934 — The Ukrainian Soviet capital moves to Kyiv"}]},{"id":"granada","name":"Granada","overview":"Granada grew through the dynastic changes of al-Andalus and became a major court and cultural center in southern Iberia under the Nasrids. The Alhambra's palaces, courtyards and water systems reflect this urban life. Conquest in 1492 changed the political and religious order; later conservation reshaped how the city's past was understood.","milestones":[{"year":1013,"date":"1013","title":"Granada under the Zirids","text":"The Zirid dynasty established a taifa state in Granada, making the city a regional seat of power and laying foundations for its later development as a capital.","polity":"Zirid Taifa of Granada","sourceIds":["granada-kingdoms"]},{"year":1238,"date":"1238","title":"The Nasrids establish their capital","text":"Muhammad I made Granada his capital, established his court in the Albaicín and began developing the Alhambra's palace city and water system.","polity":"Nasrid dynasty","sourceIds":["granada-alhambra"]},{"year":1492,"date":"1492","title":"The end of Nasrid rule","text":"Ferdinand and Isabella took control of Granada as the last Nasrid ruler surrendered the city. The Alhambra became a royal complex for the Christian monarchs.","polity":"Nasrid dynasty / Crown of Castile","sourceIds":["granada-alhambra","granada-kingdoms"]},{"year":1870,"date":"1870","title":"The Alhambra becomes a National Monument","text":"The Alhambra was declared a National Monument. Conservation and restoration of its remains gradually became central to the modern city's cultural life.","polity":"Spain","sourceIds":["granada-protection"]}],"sources":[{"id":"granada-kingdoms","title":"Centre for Andalusian Studies: The Three Kingdoms of Granada","url":"https://www.fundacioncentra.eu/publicaciones/los-tres-reinos-de-granada-ah-40","institution":"Centre for Andalusian Studies","supports":"1013 — Granada under the Zirids; 1492 — The end of Nasrid rule"},{"id":"granada-alhambra","title":"Council of the Alhambra and Generalife: History","url":"https://www.alhambra-patronato.es/descubrir/alhambra-y-generalife/historia","institution":"Council of the Alhambra and Generalife","supports":"1238 — The Nasrids establish their capital; 1492 — The end of Nasrid rule"},{"id":"granada-protection","title":"Council of the Alhambra: Key Dates in Its History, Art and Heritage","url":"https://www.alhambra-patronato.es/en/notas-prensa/a-different-view-of-the-alhambra-through-its-history-art-and-heritage-at-the-madoc","institution":"Council of the Alhambra","supports":"1870 — The Alhambra becomes a National Monument"}]},{"id":"palermo","name":"Palermo","overview":"Palermo on Sicily's north coast connected Mediterranean sea routes and passed through Roman, Byzantine, Muslim and Norman rule. Under the Norman kingdom, Latin, Greek and Islamic architecture and craftsmanship came together at court. The royal palace, chapels and cathedral preserve this history of coexisting languages and traditions.","milestones":[{"year":831,"date":"831","title":"Muslim forces capture Palermo","text":"Muslim forces from North Africa captured the city. Palermo gradually became the principal political and commercial center of Sicily under Islamic rule.","polity":"Aghlabid dynasty","sourceIds":["palermo-history"]},{"year":1072,"date":"1072","title":"The Norman conquest","text":"Norman forces captured Palermo. The kingdom later established there continued to draw on local Islamic and Byzantine cultural traditions.","polity":"Norman Sicily","sourceIds":["palermo-history","palermo-unesco"]},{"year":1130,"date":"25 December 1130","title":"Coronation of Roger II","text":"Roger II was crowned king of Sicily in Palermo Cathedral. The city became the capital of a kingdom encompassing Sicily and southern Italy.","polity":"Kingdom of Sicily","sourceIds":["palermo-history"]},{"year":1198,"date":"17 May 1198","title":"Frederick II crowned king of Sicily","text":"The young Frederick received the Sicilian crown in Palermo, before his later German kingship and imperial coronation in Rome.","polity":"Kingdom of Sicily","sourceIds":["palermo-frederick"]}],"sources":[{"id":"palermo-history","title":"Treccani Encyclopedia: History of Palermo","url":"https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/palermo_%28Enciclopedia-Italiana%29/","institution":"Treccani Encyclopedia","supports":"831 — Muslim forces capture Palermo; 1072 — The Norman conquest; 25 December 1130 — Coronation of Roger II"},{"id":"palermo-unesco","title":"UNESCO: Arab-Norman Palermo","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1487","institution":"UNESCO","supports":"1072 — The Norman conquest"},{"id":"palermo-frederick","title":"Deutsche Biographie: Frederick II","url":"https://www.deutsche-biographie.de/pnd118535765.html","institution":"Deutsche Biographie","supports":"17 May 1198 — Frederick II crowned king of Sicily"}]},{"id":"athens","name":"Athens","overview":"Athens was a major center of ancient Greek civic politics, drama and philosophy. The Acropolis records the public building projects and religious life of the fifth century BCE. After Roman, Byzantine and Ottoman rule, Athens became modern Greece's capital in the nineteenth century. Neoclassical architecture, archaeological conservation and the modern Olympics gave the ancient city new public roles.","milestones":[{"year":-447,"date":"447 BCE","title":"Construction of the Parthenon begins","text":"Athens began building the Parthenon. Construction and sculpture continued until c. 432 BCE, making the temple the centerpiece of the Acropolis building program.","polity":"Athenian city-state","sourceIds":["athens-acropolis"]},{"year":1834,"date":"1834","title":"Athens becomes modern Greece's capital","text":"Athens was chosen as the capital of the modern Greek state. New urban plans and neoclassical buildings connected its ancient remains with the national political center.","polity":"Kingdom of Greece","sourceIds":["athens-city"]},{"year":1896,"date":"1896","title":"The first modern Olympic Games","text":"Athens hosted the first modern Olympic Games, bringing the historical symbolism of ancient Greece into a new international sporting institution.","polity":"Kingdom of Greece","sourceIds":["athens-olympics"]}],"sources":[{"id":"athens-acropolis","title":"UNESCO: Acropolis, Athens","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/404/","institution":"UNESCO","supports":"447 BCE — Construction of the Parthenon begins"},{"id":"athens-city","title":"Municipality of Athens: City History","url":"https://www.cityofathens.gr/en/the-municipality-of-athens/","institution":"Municipality of Athens","supports":"1834 — Athens becomes modern Greece's capital"},{"id":"athens-olympics","title":"International Olympic Committee: Origins of the Modern Olympic Games","url":"https://www.olympics.com/ioc/faq/history-and-origin-of-the-games/why-are-the-olympic-games-held-every-four-years","institution":"International Olympic Committee","supports":"1896 — The first modern Olympic Games"}]},{"id":"rouen","name":"Rouen","overview":"The Seine connected Rouen with the Parisian hinterland and the English Channel, making it a major commercial and political center of Normandy. Rivalries among Norman dukes and the French and English crowns repeatedly changed its fortunes. The cathedral, sites associated with Joan of Arc's trial and neighborhoods rebuilt after the Second World War preserve traces of these turning points.","milestones":[{"year":1066,"date":"1066","title":"Norman ties across the Channel","text":"After William, duke of Normandy, conquered England, Rouen's political and economic links with England deepened within a system of rule spanning the Channel.","polity":"Duchy of Normandy","sourceIds":["rouen-medieval"]},{"year":1204,"date":"1204","title":"The French crown takes Rouen","text":"Philip II's forces took Rouen, and most of mainland Normandy came under French royal control, fundamentally changing the city's political ties with England.","polity":"Kingdom of France","sourceIds":["rouen-normandy"]},{"year":1431,"date":"30 May 1431","title":"Joan of Arc executed in Rouen","text":"In English-held Rouen, Joan of Arc was burned at the stake in the Old Market Square following her trial before an ecclesiastical court.","polity":"Normandy under the English crown","sourceIds":["rouen-medieval"]},{"year":1944,"date":"30 August 1944","title":"Liberation of Rouen","text":"Canadian forces liberated Rouen. War and bombing had severely damaged neighborhoods on both banks and historic buildings, leading to a period of postwar reconstruction.","polity":"Provisional Government of the French Republic","sourceIds":["rouen-war"]}],"sources":[{"id":"rouen-medieval","title":"City of Rouen: Rouen in the Middle Ages","url":"https://www.rouen.fr/fr/moyen-age","institution":"City of Rouen","supports":"1066 — Norman ties across the Channel; 30 May 1431 — Joan of Arc executed in Rouen"},{"id":"rouen-normandy","title":"Prefecture of Normandy: History of Normandy","url":"https://www.prefectures-regions.gouv.fr/normandie/Region-et-institutions/Portrait-de-la-region/Histoire/Histoire-de-la-Normandie","institution":"Prefecture of Normandy","supports":"1204 — The French crown takes Rouen"},{"id":"rouen-war","title":"City of Rouen: The Second World War","url":"https://www.rouen.fr/fr/seconde-guerre-mondiale","institution":"City of Rouen","supports":"30 August 1944 — Liberation of Rouen"}]},{"id":"orleans","name":"Orléans","overview":"River transport, bridges and fortifications made Orléans on the Loire an important link between northern and southern France. The siege and relief during the Hundred Years' War tied the city closely to Joan of Arc's memory. Industry and military functions later changed urban life, while rebuilding after wartime bombing left historic and modern neighborhoods side by side.","milestones":[{"year":1429,"date":"8 May 1429","title":"The siege of Orléans lifted","text":"Following French operations in which Joan of Arc participated, English forces ended the siege of Orléans. The victory improved the military position of Charles VII's supporters.","polity":"Kingdom of France (Charles VII's supporters)","sourceIds":["orleans-joan"]},{"year":1870,"date":"1870","title":"Orléans in the Franco-Prussian War","text":"German forces occupied Orléans during the war, as the Loire valley became a major theater of fighting. The city later developed into an important military command center.","polity":"French Third Republic","sourceIds":["orleans-archaeology"]},{"year":1944,"date":"16 August 1944","title":"US troops and the Resistance liberate the city","text":"US troops liberated Orléans with support from local Resistance forces. Bombing in 1940 and 1944 had damaged parts of the old town, and postwar rebuilding used new construction techniques.","polity":"Provisional Government of the French Republic","sourceIds":["orleans-liberation","orleans-archaeology"]}],"sources":[{"id":"orleans-joan","title":"International Joan of Arc Society: Chronology of Joan and the Hundred Years' War","url":"https://www.joanofarcsociety.org/joan-resources/chronology-of-events-in-joans-life-and-the-hundred-years-war/","institution":"International Joan of Arc Society","supports":"8 May 1429 — The siege of Orléans lifted"},{"id":"orleans-archaeology","title":"INRAP: Orléans in the Early Modern and Modern Periods","url":"https://multimedia.inrap.fr/atlas/orleans/syntheses/periodes-chronologiques/p-17785-Les-periodes-moderne-et-contemporaine.htm","institution":"French National Institute for Preventive Archaeological Research (INRAP)","supports":"1870 — Orléans in the Franco-Prussian War; 16 August 1944 — US troops and the Resistance liberate the city"},{"id":"orleans-liberation","title":"City of Orléans: The 80th Anniversary of the 1944 Liberation","url":"https://www.orleans.fr/fileadmin/orleans/MEDIA/kiosque/environnement/LIVRET-80-ANS-LIBERATION-VF.pdf","institution":"City of Orléans","supports":"16 August 1944 — US troops and the Resistance liberate the city"}]},{"id":"venice","name":"Venice","latin":"Venezia","coord":[12.3358,45.4372],"since":828,"overview":"Venice developed in the Adriatic lagoon, growing from a port closely connected to Byzantium into a maritime republic. Shipping, trading privileges and overseas bases brought wealth, while St Mark's Square and the city's palaces brought together eastern and western art. Medieval expansion and the republic's later fall shaped the history of this city on the water.","milestones":[{"year":828,"date":"828","title":"St Mark's relics arrive","text":"Two Venetian merchants brought St Mark's relics from Alexandria. The city's association with the saint became an important source of its religious and political symbolism.","polity":"Venice","sourceIds":["venice-met"]},{"year":1204,"date":"1204","title":"Venice joins the capture of Constantinople","text":"Venetian forces led by Doge Enrico Dandolo took part in the Fourth Crusade's assault on Constantinople, subsequently acquiring territory and trading bases in the eastern Mediterranean.","polity":"Republic of Venice","sourceIds":["venice-cambridge"]},{"year":1797,"date":"1797","title":"The republic ends","text":"Under military pressure from Napoleon, the centuries-old Venetian Republic came to an end. The city entered a new period of French and Austrian rivalry and rule.","polity":"Republic of Venice / French Republic","sourceIds":["venice-met"]}],"sources":[{"id":"venice-met","title":"Venice and the Islamic World, 828–1797","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/fr/essays/venice-and-the-islamic-world-828-1797","supports":"828 — St Mark's relics arrive; 1797 — The republic ends"},{"id":"venice-cambridge","title":"Venice: History of the Floating City—Chronology","institution":"Cambridge University Press","url":"https://assets.cambridge.org/97805218/83597/frontmatter/9780521883597_frontmatter.pdf","supports":"1204 — Venice joins the capture of Constantinople"}]},{"id":"genoa","name":"Genoa","latin":"Genova","coord":[8.9339,44.4056],"since":1099,"overview":"On the Ligurian coast, Genoa's port meets a densely built hillside city. Medieval merchants, nobles and communal institutions drove its maritime expansion, competing with Pisa and Venice and developing trade networks in the eastern Mediterranean. Later republican reforms and the growth of finance kept Genoa involved in the economic affairs of Europe's major powers.","milestones":[{"year":1099,"date":"c. 1099","title":"The autonomous commune takes shape","text":"Associations from the city's districts formed a common organization as the consular system developed, giving urban self-government involving merchants and nobles a more stable structure.","polity":"Commune of Genoa","sourceIds":["genoa-medieval"]},{"year":1284,"date":"6 August 1284","title":"Victory at the Battle of Meloria","text":"The Genoese fleet defeated Pisa's fleet off the Pisan coast, weakening a major maritime rival and strengthening Genoa's position in the Tyrrhenian Sea.","polity":"Republic of Genoa","sourceIds":["genoa-history"]},{"year":1528,"date":"1528","title":"Doria reorganizes the republic","text":"Andrea Doria allied with Charles V, reorganized republican institutions and introduced two-year terms for doges, opening a new phase of aristocratic rule.","polity":"Republic of Genoa","sourceIds":["genoa-doria"]}],"sources":[{"id":"genoa-medieval","title":"Genoa: City History in the Encyclopedia of Medieval Art","institution":"Treccani Encyclopedia","url":"https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/genova_%28Enciclopedia-dell%27-Arte-Medievale%29/","supports":"c. 1099 — The autonomous commune takes shape"},{"id":"genoa-history","title":"Genoa: Dictionary of History","institution":"Treccani Encyclopedia","url":"https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/genova_%28Dizionario-di-Storia%29/","supports":"6 August 1284 — Victory at the Battle of Meloria"},{"id":"genoa-doria","title":"Genoa: The Development of the City and Republic","institution":"Treccani Encyclopedia","url":"https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/genova_%28Enciclopedia-dei-ragazzi%29/","supports":"1528 — Doria reorganizes the republic"}]},{"id":"florence","name":"Florence","latin":"Firenze","coord":[11.2558,43.7696],"since":897,"overview":"Florence on the Arno grew through textiles, trade and finance, while guilds and civic government funded public buildings. The baptistery, cathedral and civic palace reflect the close ties between religious and civic life. Medici patronage and hereditary rule later transformed the balance of power inherited from the republican era.","milestones":[{"year":897,"date":"897","title":"A place of worship dedicated to St John is recorded","text":"Written evidence records a religious site here dedicated to John the Baptist. The area around the later baptistery became a lasting center of Florentine religious and civic life.","polity":"Kingdom of Italy","sourceIds":["florence-baptistery"]},{"year":1296,"date":"8 September 1296","title":"Florence Cathedral's foundation stone laid","text":"Arnolfo di Cambio led the initial design of the new cathedral. The ambitious building project expressed the city's wealth, civic identity and religious devotion.","polity":"Republic of Florence","sourceIds":["florence-cathedral"]},{"year":1532,"date":"1532","title":"Hereditary Medici ducal rule established","text":"Institutional reforms gave Alessandro de' Medici hereditary ducal status. Traditional republican bodies were reorganized as the city entered a period of ducal rule.","polity":"Duchy of Florence","sourceIds":["florence-duchy"]}],"sources":[{"id":"florence-baptistery","title":"The Baptistery: Historical Records and Archaeological Research","institution":"Opera di Santa Maria del Fiore","url":"https://duomo.firenze.it/en/opera-magazine/post/6208/how-old-is-the-baptistery-of-florence-what-do-the-legends-say-and-what-have-the-archeologist-found","supports":"897 — A place of worship dedicated to St John is recorded"},{"id":"florence-cathedral","title":"Cathedral of Santa Maria del Fiore","institution":"Opera di Santa Maria del Fiore","url":"https://duomo.firenze.it/en/discover/cathedral","supports":"8 September 1296 — Florence Cathedral's foundation stone laid"},{"id":"florence-duchy","title":"Alessandro de' Medici: The Institutional Reforms of 1532","institution":"Italian State Archives Information System (SIAS)","url":"https://sias-archivi.cultura.gov.it/cgi-bin/pagina.pl?Chiave=81042&TipoPag=prodpersona","supports":"1532 — Hereditary Medici ducal rule established"}]},{"id":"milan","name":"Milan","latin":"Milano","coord":[9.19,45.4642],"since":1162,"overview":"Agriculture, transport and crafts on the Lombard plain helped Milan become a major city of northern Italy. Its medieval commune clashed fiercely with imperial power before the Visconti and Sforza families established courtly rule. The cathedral and castle preserve the legacy of urban wealth, seigneurial power and Renaissance art.","milestones":[{"year":1162,"date":"1162","title":"Siege and destruction of the defenses","text":"Frederick I forced Milan to surrender. Its defenses were dismantled and its inhabitants compelled to move outside the walls, bringing the conflict between civic autonomy and imperial power to a climax.","polity":"Commune of Milan / Holy Roman Empire","sourceIds":["milan-history"]},{"year":1386,"date":"c. 1386","title":"Construction of Milan Cathedral begins","text":"Bishop Antonio da Saluzzo and the Visconti rulers promoted the new cathedral. Craftsmen from different regions contributed to a building project that continued for centuries.","polity":"Milan under the Visconti","sourceIds":["milan-duomo"]},{"year":1450,"date":"1450","title":"The Sforza take power","text":"Francesco Sforza became ruler of Milan, ending the Ambrosian Republic. He began rebuilding the castle as a center of ducal authority.","polity":"Duchy of Milan","sourceIds":["milan-castle"]}],"sources":[{"id":"milan-history","title":"Milan: Dictionary of History","institution":"Treccani Encyclopedia","url":"https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/milano_%28Dizionario-di-Storia%29/","supports":"1162 — Siege and destruction of the defenses"},{"id":"milan-duomo","title":"Milan Cathedral: Architecture and History","institution":"Veneranda Fabbrica del Duomo di Milano","url":"https://www.duomomilano.it/en/art-and-culture/the-cathedral/","supports":"c. 1386 — Construction of Milan Cathedral begins"},{"id":"milan-castle","title":"The Castle of the Visconti and Sforza","institution":"City of Milan, Sforza Castle Museums","url":"https://www2.comune.milano.it/web/castello-sforzesco/scopri-il-castello/la-storia-del-castello/il-castello-dei-visconti-e-degli-sforza","supports":"1450 — The Sforza take power"}]},{"id":"naples","name":"Naples","latin":"Napoli","coord":[14.2681,40.8518],"since":1139,"overview":"The Bay of Naples connected southern Italy with Mediterranean shipping routes, while the ancient Greek street plan continued to shape urban development. The medieval duchy gradually escaped Byzantine control before joining the Norman kingdom. Its university, palaces and castles helped make Naples a major political and cultural center under Angevin and Aragonese rule.","milestones":[{"year":1139,"date":"1139","title":"Incorporated into the Norman kingdom","text":"Naples finally surrendered to the Norman rulers. The long-autonomous duchy joined the Kingdom of Sicily, bringing the city under a unified southern Italian monarchy.","polity":"Kingdom of Sicily","sourceIds":["naples-history"]},{"year":1224,"date":"5 June 1224","title":"Frederick II founds the university","text":"Frederick II established a university in Naples by charter to train people for law, administration and other fields within his kingdom.","polity":"Kingdom of Sicily","sourceIds":["naples-university"]},{"year":1442,"date":"1442","title":"The Aragonese crown takes the city","text":"Alfonso V captured Naples, ending Angevin rule. The city subsequently became an important seat of the Aragonese court in Italy.","polity":"Kingdom of Naples (Aragonese dynasty)","sourceIds":["naples-university","naples-unesco"]}],"sources":[{"id":"naples-history","title":"Naples: The Medieval Duchy and Norman Conquest","institution":"Treccani Encyclopedia","url":"https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/napoli_%28Dizionario-di-Storia%29/","supports":"1139 — Incorporated into the Norman kingdom"},{"id":"naples-university","title":"University History and the Foundation Charter of 1224","institution":"University of Naples Federico II","url":"https://www.dises.unina.it/web/guest/unina-international/about-us/history","supports":"5 June 1224 — Frederick II founds the university; 1442 — The Aragonese crown takes the city"},{"id":"naples-unesco","title":"Historic Centre of Naples","institution":"UNESCO","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/726/","supports":"1442 — The Aragonese crown takes the city"}]},{"id":"prague","name":"Prague","latin":"Praha","coord":[14.4208,50.0875],"since":973,"overview":"Prague grew along the Vltava, with its castle, markets and church institutions forming Bohemia's political core. The university and urban development under Charles IV extended its influence in central Europe. Religious reform and disputes over royal authority later made Prague a center of conflict, while the defenestration at the castle helped spark a European war.","milestones":[{"year":973,"date":"973","title":"The bishopric of Prague established","text":"The new bishopric strengthened Prague's religious standing. Its cathedral school and church institutions gradually became important parts of Bohemia's intellectual and administrative life.","polity":"Duchy of Bohemia","sourceIds":["prague-bishopric"]},{"year":1348,"date":"7 April 1348","title":"Charles University founded","text":"Charles IV issued the university's founding charter, establishing faculties of liberal arts, medicine, law and theology and making Prague a major center of learning in central Europe.","polity":"Kingdom of Bohemia","sourceIds":["prague-university"]},{"year":1618,"date":"1618","title":"The Defenestration of Prague","text":"Bohemian nobles opposing royal policies threw two governors and a secretary from a castle window. The incident triggered a revolt and became a catalyst for the Thirty Years' War.","polity":"Kingdom of Bohemia / Habsburg Monarchy","sourceIds":["prague-defenestration"]}],"sources":[{"id":"prague-bishopric","title":"The Prague Cathedral Chapter and Its School","institution":"St Vitus Cathedral, Prague","url":"https://www.katedralasvatehovita.cz/en/about-the-cathedral/metropolitan-chapter/","supports":"973 — The bishopric of Prague established"},{"id":"prague-university","title":"Key Dates in the History of Charles University","institution":"Charles University","url":"https://cuni.cz/UKEN-115.html","supports":"7 April 1348 — Charles University founded"},{"id":"prague-defenestration","title":"The Old Royal Palace and the Defenestration of 1618","institution":"Prague Castle, Office of the President of the Czech Republic","url":"https://www.hrad.cz/en/prague-castle-for-visitors/objects-for-visitors/old-royal-palace-10332","supports":"1618 — The Defenestration of Prague"}]},{"id":"krakow","name":"Kraków","latin":"Kraków","coord":[19.945,50.0647],"since":1000,"overview":"Kraków developed beside the Vistula and Wawel Hill, serving for centuries as a royal, religious and commercial center of Poland. Its university and court attracted scholars and artists, while the Main Market Square preserved the framework of the medieval city plan. Shifting political centers and partitions changed its status, but royal ceremonies and academic traditions remained influential.","milestones":[{"year":1000,"date":"1000","title":"The bishopric of Kraków established","text":"Kraków became the seat of a new bishopric. Church buildings and royal settlements developed together on Wawel Hill, strengthening the city's place in the early Polish state.","polity":"Piast Poland","sourceIds":["krakow-city"]},{"year":1364,"date":"1364","title":"Kraków University founded","text":"Casimir III founded the Kraków Academy, the basis of the later Jagiellonian University, giving the city an enduring role in education and scholarship.","polity":"Kingdom of Poland","sourceIds":["krakow-university"]},{"year":1795,"date":"1795","title":"A change of rule in the Third Partition","text":"The Third Partition of Poland assigned Kraków to the Habsburgs, bringing this traditional center of Polish royalty into an Austrian political order.","polity":"Habsburg Monarchy","sourceIds":["krakow-archive"]}],"sources":[{"id":"krakow-city","title":"History of Kraków","institution":"City of Kraków","url":"https://www.krakow.pl/nasze_miasto/1115,artykul,historia.html","supports":"1000 — The bishopric of Kraków established"},{"id":"krakow-university","title":"The History of the City and University","institution":"Jagiellonian University Medical College","url":"https://cm-uj.krakow.pl/collegium-medicum/historia/","supports":"1364 — Kraków University founded"},{"id":"krakow-archive","title":"The Kraków Archives and a Chronology of the City","institution":"National Archives in Kraków","url":"https://ank.gov.pl/en/about-us/history-of-the-national-archives-in-krakow/","supports":"1795 — A change of rule in the Third Partition"}]},{"id":"buda","name":"Buda","modern":"Budapest","latin":"Buda","coord":[19.0399,47.4981],"since":1148,"overview":"Early Buda occupied the area now called Óbuda. As a new fortified town rose on Castle Hill in the thirteenth century, the name Buda gradually shifted to it. Danube trade and the royal court fostered prosperity, while Ottoman occupation changed the city's role. Buda, Pest and Óbuda form the historic core of modern Budapest.","milestones":[{"year":1148,"date":"1148","title":"A church charter from early Buda","text":"A charter of Géza II records the religious community of St Peter. Buda then lay in present-day Óbuda, where ecclesiastical and royal settlements formed its early core.","polity":"Kingdom of Hungary","sourceIds":["buda-old"]},{"year":1408,"date":"1408","title":"Sigismund moves his court to Buda","text":"Sigismund moved his court and government offices to Buda on Castle Hill. Palace building and court demand further strengthened the city's political and commercial life.","polity":"Kingdom of Hungary","sourceIds":["buda-medieval"]},{"year":1541,"date":"29 August 1541","title":"Ottoman forces take control of Buda","text":"Suleiman I's forces seized the gates and strategic positions, then established Ottoman rule. Hungary's royal capital became a regional administrative and military center of the empire.","polity":"Ottoman Empire","sourceIds":["buda-ottoman"]}],"sources":[{"id":"buda-old","title":"Dóra Mojzsis: Buildings and Documents from the Center of Medieval Óbuda","institution":"Óbudai Anziksz, Óbuda's Municipal Cultural Magazine","url":"https://obudaianziksz.hu/benyone-dr-mojzsis-dora-a-fo-ter-epuletei-a-kozepkorban/","supports":"1148 — A church charter from early Buda"},{"id":"buda-medieval","title":"András Végh: The History of Buda to 1541","institution":"Hungarian Research Centre for the Humanities, Buda Castle Project","url":"https://budavar.abtk.hu/en/the-history-of-the-site/the-history-of-buda-to-1541","supports":"1408 — Sigismund moves his court to Buda"},{"id":"buda-ottoman","title":"Károly Magyar: Buda in the Ottoman Era","institution":"Hungarian Research Centre for the Humanities, Buda Castle Project","url":"https://budavar.abtk.hu/en/the-history-of-the-site/buda-in-the-turkish-era.html","supports":"29 August 1541 — Ottoman forces take control of Buda"}]},{"id":"lisbon","name":"Lisbon","latin":"Lisboa","coord":[-9.1393,38.7223],"since":1147,"overview":"Lisbon stands at the Tagus estuary, linking the hinterland with Atlantic shipping routes. After passing from Islamic to Portuguese royal rule, it developed as a center of the court, commerce and higher learning. Early modern maritime expansion widened its global connections, while the eighteenth-century earthquake and planned reconstruction profoundly changed the waterfront city.","milestones":[{"year":1147,"date":"1147","title":"Afonso I captures Lisbon","text":"With help from northern European crusaders, Afonso I captured Lisbon, ending Islamic rule and incorporating the important port into the Kingdom of Portugal.","polity":"Kingdom of Portugal","sourceIds":["lisbon-archive"]},{"year":1290,"date":"1290","title":"A Portuguese university is founded here","text":"Dinis I issued a foundation charter for a university in Lisbon. It later moved several times between Lisbon and Coimbra before settling permanently in Coimbra in 1537.","polity":"Kingdom of Portugal","sourceIds":["lisbon-university"]},{"year":1755,"date":"1 November 1755","title":"Earthquake, tsunami and reconstruction","text":"A powerful earthquake, tsunami and fires devastated Lisbon. Pombaline reconstruction reshaped the lower town with a regular street grid and new building systems.","polity":"Kingdom of Portugal","sourceIds":["lisbon-earthquake","lisbon-archive"]}],"sources":[{"id":"lisbon-archive","title":"The Municipal History of Lisbon","institution":"Lisbon Municipal Archives","url":"https://arquivomunicipal.lisboa.pt/sobre-nos/historia/","supports":"1147 — Afonso I captures Lisbon; 1 November 1755 — Earthquake, tsunami and reconstruction"},{"id":"lisbon-university","title":"University History from the Thirteenth to Sixteenth Centuries","institution":"University of Coimbra","url":"https://www.uc.pt/sobrenos/historia/xiii-a-xvi/","supports":"1290 — A Portuguese university is founded here"},{"id":"lisbon-earthquake","title":"The Earthquake of 1 November 1755","institution":"City of Lisbon, Office of Lisbon Studies","url":"https://geo.lisboa.pt/atividades-e-difusao/investigacao/detalhe/o-terramoto-de-1-de-novembro-de-1755","supports":"1 November 1755 — Earthquake, tsunami and reconstruction"}]},{"id":"cordoba","name":"Córdoba","latin":"Córdoba","coord":[-4.7794,37.8882],"since":756,"overview":"Córdoba on the Guadalquivir was a political and cultural center of al-Andalus. The Umayyad court supported mosques, palace cities, libraries and crafts, while the city maintained wide Mediterranean connections. Thirteenth-century conquest changed religious and royal authority, and the Great Mosque still preserves layers of this history.","milestones":[{"year":756,"date":"756","title":"The Umayyad emirate established","text":"Abd al-Rahman I established an emirate in al-Andalus independent of Abbasid political rule, consolidating his power around Córdoba.","polity":"Emirate of Córdoba","sourceIds":["cordoba-umayyad"]},{"year":929,"date":"929","title":"Abd al-Rahman III proclaims himself caliph","text":"The ruler adopted the title of caliph, opening a new phase in the Córdoba court's power and its patronage of scholarship and the arts.","polity":"Caliphate of Córdoba","sourceIds":["cordoba-umayyad"]},{"year":1236,"date":"1236","title":"Ferdinand III takes the city","text":"Ferdinand III conquered Córdoba. The Great Mosque became a Christian cathedral, and the city passed under the Crown of Castile.","polity":"Crown of Castile","sourceIds":["cordoba-unesco"]}],"sources":[{"id":"cordoba-umayyad","title":"Art and Politics in Umayyad Iberia, 711–1031","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/ru/essays/the-art-of-the-umayyad-period-in-spain-711-1031","supports":"756 — The Umayyad emirate established; 929 — Abd al-Rahman III proclaims himself caliph"},{"id":"cordoba-unesco","title":"Historic Centre of Córdoba","institution":"UNESCO","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/313","supports":"1236 — Ferdinand III takes the city"}]},{"id":"toledo","name":"Toledo","latin":"Toledo","coord":[-4.0245,39.8628],"since":1085,"overview":"Toledo occupies high ground encircled by the Tagus. It was successively a center of the Visigothic kingdom, an important city of al-Andalus and a Castilian city. Churches, surviving mosque buildings and synagogues reflect the histories of different religious communities. Cathedral construction and the later departure of the royal court chart shifts in ecclesiastical power and royal politics.","milestones":[{"year":1085,"date":"1085","title":"Alfonso VI takes Toledo","text":"Alfonso VI, king of León and Castile, took the city, changing the balance of power in central Iberia and making Toledo an important base of Christian royal authority.","polity":"Crowns of León and Castile","sourceIds":["toledo-unesco","toledo-cathedral"]},{"year":1226,"date":"1226","title":"The Gothic cathedral begun","text":"Supported by Ferdinand III and Archbishop Rodrigo Jiménez de Rada, work began on a new cathedral that would become the city's most important religious building.","polity":"Kingdom of Castile","sourceIds":["toledo-cathedral"]},{"year":1561,"date":"1561","title":"The royal court moves to Madrid","text":"Philip II chose Madrid as the court's permanent residence. Toledo lost important political functions, increasing the relative importance of church institutions in city life.","polity":"Spanish Habsburg Monarchy","sourceIds":["toledo-unesco"]}],"sources":[{"id":"toledo-unesco","title":"Historic City of Toledo","institution":"UNESCO","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/379/","supports":"1085 — Alfonso VI takes Toledo; 1561 — The royal court moves to Madrid"},{"id":"toledo-cathedral","title":"The Cathedral's Eighth Centenary: The Conquest of 1085 and Construction from 1226","institution":"Toledo Cathedral","url":"https://www.catedralprimada.es/actualidad/la-catedral-primada-presenta-los-actos-de-su-octavo-centenario/","supports":"1085 — Alfonso VI takes Toledo; 1226 — The Gothic cathedral begun"}]},{"id":"novgorod","name":"Novgorod","latin":"Velikiy Novgorod","coord":[31.2758,58.5215],"since":1045,"overview":"Novgorod on the Volkhov was an important link between the interior of Rus' and Baltic trade. Bishops, nobles, merchants and assemblies all participated in politics. Churches, frescoes and birch-bark letters preserve rich records of urban life. Its institutions of self-government evolved over time before coming to an end with Moscow's expansion.","milestones":[{"year":1045,"date":"1045","title":"Construction of St Sophia Cathedral begins","text":"Vladimir, son of Yaroslav, commissioned the stone cathedral of St Sophia. It later became central to Novgorod's religious life and civic identity.","polity":"Rus'","sourceIds":["novgorod-cathedral"]},{"year":1136,"date":"1136","title":"Prince Vsevolod expelled","text":"During a political conflict, the townspeople imprisoned and expelled Vsevolod and invited other princes to rule. The episode was a major step in limiting princely power and developing republican institutions.","polity":"Novgorod","sourceIds":["novgorod-scholarship","novgorod-chronology"]},{"year":1478,"date":"1478","title":"Annexed by Moscow","text":"Ivan III forced Novgorod to accept Moscow's rule, ending the city's centuries of political autonomy.","polity":"Grand Principality of Moscow","sourceIds":["novgorod-chronology"]}],"sources":[{"id":"novgorod-cathedral","title":"St Sophia Cathedral in Novgorod","institution":"Novgorod State Museum-Reserve","url":"https://novgorodmuseum.ru/muzei/sofijskij-sobor","supports":"1045 — Construction of St Sophia Cathedral begins"},{"id":"novgorod-scholarship","title":"The Chronicle Entry of 1136 and the Formation of Novgorod's Autonomy","institution":"Slověne academic journal","url":"https://www.slovene.ru/ojs/index.php/slovene/article/view/585","supports":"1136 — Prince Vsevolod expelled"},{"id":"novgorod-chronology","title":"Chronology of Novgorod's Political History","institution":"Daniel Waugh, University of Washington","url":"https://faculty.washington.edu/dwaugh/hstam443/n-chron.html","supports":"1136 — Prince Vsevolod expelled; 1478 — Annexed by Moscow"}]}],"polities":[{"id":"roman-empire","name":"Roman Empire","aliases":["Roman Empire","Western Roman Empire"],"matchPattern":"^(Roman Empire|Western Roman Empire)$","periodLabel":"From 27 BCE; 476 is the conventional endpoint of the western empire","center":"Rome; the later empire had several imperial residences. The western court moved from Milan to Ravenna, while Constantinople became the eastern center.","overview":"Augustus established the Principate after the civil wars, and Rome developed an empire around the Mediterranean. Provinces, cities, armies, and trade connected Europe, North Africa, and western Asia. Eastern and western courts later shared its government; the eastern Roman state survived the collapse of imperial rule in the west.","institutions":[{"title":"Princeps and Senate","text":"The early empire retained the forms of republican institutions, including the Senate, while emperors controlled the army and key appointments."},{"title":"Cities and communications","text":"Provincial cities, roads, and sea routes carried goods, people, and artistic traditions across the Mediterranean."}],"milestones":[{"year":-27,"title":"Augustus receives his title","text":"The Senate granted Octavian the title Augustus, conventionally marking the beginning of imperial rule.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":117,"title":"The age of Trajan","text":"The empire reached its greatest extent near the end of Trajan's reign; some newly conquered eastern territories were subsequently relinquished.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":330,"title":"Constantinople inaugurated","text":"Constantine developed his new capital on the Bosporus into a major imperial political center.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":476,"title":"Western emperor deposed","text":"Odoacer deposed Romulus Augustulus; Julius Nepos retained a claim to the western imperial throne until 480.","sourceIndexes":[2]}],"sources":[{"title":"The Roman Empire (27 B.C.–393 A.D.)","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/the-roman-empire-27-b-c-393-a-d","supports":"Augustus, imperial institutions and culture, and Constantinople"},{"title":"Imperial Rome: visit resource","institution":"The British Museum","url":"https://www.britishmuseum.org/sites/default/files/2019-09/Visit_Imperial_Rome_KS2b.pdf","supports":"The beginning of imperial rule and the empire's greatest extent under Trajan"},{"title":"Dethronement of Romulus Augustulus","institution":"Hungarian National Museum","url":"https://seuso.mnm.hu/en/the-seuso-treasuer/chronology/dethronement-romulus-augustulus","supports":"The events of 476 and Julius Nepos's imperial claim"}]},{"id":"byzantine-empire","name":"Byzantine Empire","aliases":["Byzantine Empire","Eastern Roman Empire"],"matchPattern":"^(Byzantine Empire|Eastern Roman Empire)$","periodLabel":"Eastern Roman continuity; the empire at Constantinople lasted until 1453","center":"Constantinople. The Latin Empire held the city from 1204 to 1261, while Roman successor states emerged in Nicaea and elsewhere.","overview":"Byzantium continued the Roman Empire in the eastern Mediterranean, and its inhabitants identified as Romans. Centered on Constantinople, it combined Roman political traditions, Greek culture, and Christianity. Despite repeated territorial losses, its legal, artistic, and religious influence reached deep into the Balkans and Rus'.","institutions":[{"title":"Empire and themes","text":"Themes were important provincial administrative and military units in the middle Byzantine period; their organization changed over time."},{"title":"Images and architecture","text":"Icons, mosaics, and domed churches connected the religious life of courts, monasteries, and cities."}],"milestones":[{"year":537,"title":"Hagia Sophia completed","text":"Hagia Sophia, rebuilt under Justinian, was consecrated.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1204,"title":"Fourth Crusade takes the capital","text":"Crusaders occupied and sacked Constantinople. The Latin Empire was established, fragmenting the empire's political territory.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1261,"title":"Constantinople recovered","text":"Forces from Nicaea recaptured the capital, restoring imperial rule there under the Palaiologan dynasty.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1453,"title":"Fall of Constantinople","text":"On May 29, the Ottoman army of Mehmed II captured the city.","sourceIndexes":[2]}],"sources":[{"title":"Byzantium (ca. 330–1453)","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/byzantium-ca-330-1453","supports":"Roman continuity, themes and culture, and the events of 1204 and 1261"},{"title":"Hagia Sophia (532–37)","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/hagia-sophia-532-37","supports":"Rebuilding under Justinian and the consecration in 537"},{"title":"The last day of Constantinople","institution":"The British Library","url":"https://www.bl.uk/stories/blogs/posts/the-last-day-of-constantinople","supports":"The fall of Constantinople on May 29, 1453"}]},{"id":"carolingian-empire","name":"Carolingian Empire","aliases":["Carolingian Empire"],"matchPattern":"^Carolingian Empire$","periodLabel":"8th–9th centuries; imperial coronation in 800, partition in 843","center":"The court moved between royal residences; Aachen became Charlemagne's principal residence in his later years and an important ceremonial center.","overview":"After taking the Frankish throne, the Carolingians expanded their rule through conquest, aristocratic cooperation, and church networks. Charlemagne was crowned emperor in Rome in 800, and his court promoted educational, scribal, and liturgical reforms. The partition at Verdun in 843 reshaped western Europe, while the dynasty continued in several kingdoms.","institutions":[{"title":"Kingship and Church","text":"Bishops, monasteries, and local nobles shared in government; imperial journeys and assemblies sustained political relationships."},{"title":"Carolingian Renaissance","text":"Courts and monasteries encouraged Latin learning, manuscript copying, and the study of classical texts, establishing enduring scribal traditions."}],"milestones":[{"year":751,"title":"Pepin takes the Frankish throne","text":"Pepin III became king, replacing the Merovingians with the Carolingian dynasty.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":800,"title":"Charlemagne crowned emperor","text":"On December 25, Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne in Rome.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":814,"title":"Louis the Pious succeeds","text":"Charlemagne died, and his son Louis inherited the empire.","sourceIndexes":[0,2]},{"year":843,"title":"Partition at Verdun","text":"Louis's three sons divided rule over West Francia, Middle Francia, and East Francia.","sourceIndexes":[0]}],"sources":[{"title":"France, 500–1000 A.D.","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/de/toah/ht/06/euwf","supports":"Dynastic change, the coronation of 800, succession in 814, and partition in 843"},{"title":"Central Europe, 500–1000 A.D.","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://artistproject.metmuseum.org/toah/ht/06/euwc.html","supports":"Aachen, the revival of classical learning, and the arts"},{"title":"Inventing the Carolingian empire: politics and government, 800–840","institution":"Cambridge University Press","url":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/carolingian-world/inventing-the-carolingian-empire-politics-and-government-800840/8745B6150B7D0AA422798A7254F3B7B8","supports":"Aachen as a principal palace, succession in 814, and political organization"}]},{"id":"holy-roman-empire","name":"Holy Roman Empire","aliases":["Holy Roman Empire"],"matchPattern":"^Holy Roman Empire$","periodLabel":"Conventionally dated 962–1806","center":"There was no single capital throughout its history. Medieval emperors governed through an itinerant court, visiting royal palaces, episcopal cities, and assembly sites.","overview":"The empire grew from East Frankish kingship and the Roman imperial tradition. It encompassed secular principalities, ecclesiastical territories, and imperial cities. Emperors and the Imperial Estates upheld a shared political and legal order amid diverse regional rights and languages. Its changing territories extended across several present-day European countries.","institutions":[{"title":"Elective kingship","text":"The Golden Bull of 1356 established key procedures for electing the King of the Romans through seven electors."},{"title":"Estates and justice","text":"Princes and imperial cities participated in the Imperial Diet. The reforms of 1495 created the Imperial Chamber Court, strengthening a shared legal order."}],"milestones":[{"year":962,"title":"Otto I crowned emperor","text":"Otto I received the imperial crown in Rome, conventionally regarded as a key stage in the empire's formation.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1356,"title":"Golden Bull issued","text":"Charles IV defined the electors' position and the rules governing royal elections.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1495,"title":"Imperial reforms at Worms","text":"The Imperial Diet proclaimed the Perpetual Public Peace and established the Imperial Chamber Court.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1806,"title":"End of the empire","text":"On August 6, Francis II renounced the title of Holy Roman Emperor and released the empire's members from their associated obligations.","sourceIndexes":[2]}],"sources":[{"title":"Holy Roman Empire (of the German Nation)","institution":"University of Oldenburg, OME Encyclopedia","url":"https://ome-lexikon.uni-oldenburg.de/en/countries/holy-roman-empire-of-the-german-nation","supports":"Periodization, constituent territories, electors, and the reforms of 1495"},{"title":"Itinerant Kingship and Royal Monasteries in Early Medieval Germany, c.936–1075","institution":"Cambridge University Press","url":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/itinerant-kingship-and-royal-monasteries-in-early-medieval-germany-c9361075/DFF0A6310D74B0FF2B1DCF1E9DCFEE06","supports":"The itinerant court and government without a fixed capital"},{"title":"Declaration of Emperor Francis II, 6 August 1806","institution":"German Historical Institute, GHDI","url":"https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/the-holy-roman-empire-1648-1815/ghdi%3Adocument-5355","supports":"The declaration ending the empire in 1806"}]},{"id":"kingdom-of-france","name":"Kingdom of France","aliases":["Kingdom of France"],"matchPattern":"^Kingdom of France$","periodLabel":"The growth of royal power · 10th–14th centuries","center":"Paris was an important center of the Capetian royal domain. Royal administrative and judicial institutions increasingly concentrated there in the 12th and 13th centuries.","overview":"The early Capetian kings directly controlled a limited royal domain, while powerful dukes and counts ruled much of the kingdom. Inheritance, marriage, war, and administrative growth expanded royal power, especially under Philip II and Louis IX. Paris also developed into a center of learning, religion, and art.","institutions":[{"title":"Royal domain and feudal rights","text":"The king's position throughout the kingdom differed from his direct government of the royal domain; princely rights endured in the regions."},{"title":"Gothic art","text":"Architecture, sculpture, and stained glass at Saint-Denis, Chartres, and Paris reflect patronage by the monarchy, Church, and towns."}],"milestones":[{"year":987,"title":"The Capetian dynasty begins","text":"Hugh Capet took the throne; his family and its branches would rule France for centuries.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1214,"title":"Battle of Bouvines","text":"Philip II defeated a coalition led by Otto IV, strengthening the monarchy's political position.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1226,"title":"Louis IX becomes king","text":"Religious, scholarly, and artistic patronage in Paris developed further during his reign.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1328,"title":"The Valois branch succeeds","text":"Philip VI became king of France, transferring the throne to the Valois branch of the Capetian family.","sourceIndexes":[0]}],"sources":[{"title":"France, 1000–1400 A.D.","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/ht/07/euwf.html","supports":"The Capetians, royal power and Paris, Gothic culture, and the events of 1226 and 1328"},{"title":"Crown and Government","institution":"Cambridge University Press, The New Cambridge Medieval History","url":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/new-cambridge-medieval-history/crown-and-government/584AF3E2325ECA85DBA4A5F3692E823A","supports":"Philip II's administrative expansion and the turning point of 1214"},{"title":"Capetian France 987–1328","institution":"Routledge","url":"https://www.routledge.com/Capetian-France-9871328/Hallamah2-Westah2/p/book/9781138132078","supports":"The development of Capetian kingship and political institutions"}]},{"id":"kingdom-of-england","name":"Kingdom of England and the Angevin Lands","aliases":["Kingdom of England","Angevin Empire"],"matchPattern":"^(Kingdom of England|Angevin Empire)$","periodLabel":"The Angevin kings · 1154–1216","center":"The royal court traveled between England and the continental territories. Westminster, Winchester, Rouen, and the Loire Valley each played important roles.","overview":"Henry II held the English crown alongside distinct rights in Normandy, Anjou, Aquitaine, and other territories, a collection later called the Angevin Empire. Its lands retained different institutions and political relationships. Royal justice expanded in England, while wars across the Channel and financial pressures intensified conflict between the crown and nobles.","institutions":[{"title":"One monarch, separate governments","text":"The English kingdom and continental duchies and counties shared a dynasty but retained different legal positions and forms of government."},{"title":"Itinerant justice","text":"Henry II sent judges on circuit, encouraging the development of royal courts and English common law."}],"milestones":[{"year":1154,"title":"Henry II succeeds","text":"The English crown joined the Angevin family's continental possessions, beginning a new phase of rule across the Channel.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1189,"title":"Richard I succeeds","text":"Henry II died, and Richard I inherited the English crown and dynastic lands.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1204,"title":"John loses Normandy","text":"Philip II of France's conquests weakened Angevin power on the continent.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1215,"title":"Magna Carta","text":"At Runnymede, John accepted the charter demanded by his barons; its provisions endured and changed through later reissues.","sourceIndexes":[2]}],"sources":[{"title":"Henry II 'Curtmantle' (r. 1154–1189)","institution":"The Royal Family, historical resources","url":"https://www.royal.uk/henry-ii","supports":"Henry II's multiple titles, itinerant government, judicial reforms, and succession in 1189"},{"title":"The Sieges of Dover","institution":"English Heritage","url":"https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/dover-castle/history-and-stories/the-sieges-of-dover/","supports":"Continental losses in 1204 and financial and political conflict"},{"title":"Magna Carta","institution":"The National Archives, UK","url":"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/explore-the-collection/explore-by-time-period/medieval/magna-carta/","supports":"The charter of 1215, existing rights, and later reissues"}]},{"id":"papal-states","name":"Papal States","aliases":["Papal States"],"matchPattern":"^Papal States$","periodLabel":"Formed in the 8th century; Rome lost in 1870","center":"Rome was the principal political and religious center. During the papacy's long residence at Avignon in the 14th century, representatives and local powers governed its Italian territories.","overview":"The Papal States comprised territories ruled by the pope as a temporal sovereign, chiefly in central Italy. Frankish territorial donations laid their early foundations; boundaries and effective control later changed repeatedly. The papacy's universal religious authority was distinct from its territorial government in Italy.","institutions":[{"title":"Spiritual and temporal authority","text":"The pope was both head of the Church and a territorial ruler; papal representatives and local institutions governed his lands."},{"title":"Cities and papal government","text":"Cities, nobles, and papal officials shaped political life. Rome and Avignon also became important centers of artistic patronage."}],"milestones":[{"year":756,"title":"Donation of Pepin","text":"The Frankish king Pepin's territorial donation strengthened the foundations of papal temporal rule in central Italy.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1309,"title":"Papacy settles at Avignon","text":"Clement V took up residence at Avignon, beginning the papacy's long stay in southern France.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1377,"title":"Gregory XI returns to Rome","text":"The pope entered Rome in January, ending the preceding long period of residence centered on Avignon.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1870,"title":"Rome annexed to Italy","text":"Italian troops entered Rome, ending the Papal States' territorial rule in Italy.","sourceIndexes":[0,2]}],"sources":[{"title":"Papal States (Italia)","institution":"Museum of New Zealand Te Papa Tongarewa","url":"https://collections.tepapa.govt.nz/place/2719100","supports":"The Donation of Pepin, the territorial nature of the Papal States, and the 756–1870 period"},{"title":"Avignon Papacy","institution":"Oxford University Press","url":"https://academic.oup.com/reference/62416/reference-article-abstract/583120998","supports":"Residence at Avignon from 1309 and the return to Rome in 1377"},{"title":"The International Legal Status of the Vatican/Holy See Complex","institution":"European Journal of International Law / Oxford University Press","url":"https://academic.oup.com/ejil/article/26/4/927/2599610","supports":"The pope's dual role, the Papal States, and the end of territorial rule in 1870"}]},{"id":"republic-of-venice","name":"Republic of Venice","aliases":["Republic of Venice","Venetian Areas"],"matchPattern":"^(Republic of Venice|Venetian Areas)$","periodLabel":"Developed during the Middle Ages; the republic ended in 1797","center":"Venice; the Doge's Palace housed the head of state as well as deliberative and administrative institutions.","overview":"Venice grew into a Mediterranean power through its lagoon port and maritime trade. Connecting western Europe, Byzantium, and the Islamic world, it maintained commercial networks and overseas outposts. The doge governed alongside aristocratic councils, while the republic's wealth supported architecture, painting, and fine craftsmanship.","institutions":[{"title":"Doge and councils","text":"The doge was an elected head of state whose powers were constrained by the republic's aristocratic institutions."},{"title":"Maritime exchange","text":"Trade with the eastern Mediterranean brought goods, techniques, and visual traditions, leaving clear traces around Saint Mark's."}],"milestones":[{"year":828,"title":"Relics of Saint Mark arrive","text":"Venetian merchants brought Saint Mark's relics from Alexandria, making them central to the city's religious identity.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1204,"title":"Fourth Crusade and overseas expansion","text":"After the capture of Constantinople, Venice expanded its outposts and rights in the eastern Mediterranean.","sourceIndexes":[2]},{"year":1797,"title":"End of the republic","text":"In May, the Great Council ended the old republican government, closing Venice's long tradition of political independence.","sourceIndexes":[2]}],"sources":[{"title":"Venice and the Islamic World, 828–1797","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/fr/essays/venice-and-the-islamic-world-828-1797","supports":"The relics of 828, maritime trade, and cultural exchange in art"},{"title":"Doge","institution":"The National Gallery, London","url":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/glossary/doge","supports":"Election of the doge and the aristocratic republic"},{"title":"VENETIA 1600: Births and rebirths","institution":"Fondazione Musei Civici di Venezia / Doge's Palace","url":"https://palazzoducale.visitmuve.it/de/ausstellung/exhibition-venetia-1600/","supports":"Mediterranean expansion in 1204 and the end of the republic in 1797"}]},{"id":"kingdom-of-sicily","name":"Kingdom of Sicily","aliases":["Kingdom of Sicily"],"matchPattern":"^Kingdom of Sicily$","periodLabel":"Founded in 1130; island and mainland kingdoms divided after 1282","center":"Palermo was the center under the Normans. After the division, the island and the mainland kingdom centered on Naples developed separately.","overview":"Roger II united the Norman conquests in Sicily and southern Italy as a kingdom. Its court drew on Greek, Arabic, and Latin traditions, developing multilingual administration and distinctive art. The Hohenstaufens took the throne in 1194; the revolt against Angevin rule in 1282 transformed relations between island and mainland.","institutions":[{"title":"A multicultural court","text":"Palermo's royal palace and Palatine Chapel combined Latin, Byzantine, and Islamic artistic traditions."},{"title":"Dynasty and kingdom","text":"The throne passed to the Hohenstaufens in 1194, while the kingdom and its administrative traditions continued to develop."}],"milestones":[{"year":1130,"title":"Roger II becomes king","text":"The Norman territories of Sicily and southern Italy formed a new kingdom.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1194,"title":"Hohenstaufen succession","text":"Henry VI conquered the kingdom, ending Norman dynastic rule.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1282,"title":"Sicilian Vespers","text":"A revolt against Angevin rule broke out on the island, sparking war between the Angevins and Aragon.","sourceIndexes":[2]}],"sources":[{"title":"The Norman Kingdom of Sicily: front matter and introduction","institution":"Cambridge University Press","url":"https://assets.cambridge.org/97805212/62842/frontmatter/9780521262842_frontmatter.pdf","supports":"The kingdom's foundation in 1130 and dynastic change in 1194"},{"title":"Arab-Norman Palermo and the Cathedral Churches of Cefalú and Monreale","institution":"UNESCO","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1487/","supports":"Palermo's royal palace and the combination of Latin, Byzantine, and Islamic art"},{"title":"The Conca d'Oro","institution":"Sicilian Regional Department of Cultural Heritage","url":"https://www2.regione.sicilia.it/beniculturali/dirbenicult/info/pubblicazioni/lemappedeltesoro/le_mappe_del_tesoro/volume%2014%20ENG%20low.pdf","supports":"The revolt of 1282 and the Angevin–Aragonese war"}]},{"id":"kingdom-of-hungary","name":"Kingdom of Hungary","aliases":["Kingdom of Hungary"],"matchPattern":"^Kingdom of Hungary$","periodLabel":"Christian kingdom founded around 1000 · The Árpád period","center":"Esztergom, Székesfehérvár, and Óbuda formed the early core of royal power. Kings traveled between palaces, castles, and episcopal residences.","overview":"Stephen I established a Latin Christian kingdom whose authority spread through the Carpathian Basin through castles, counties, and church organization. Árpád kings maintained complex relations with nobles, the Church, and neighboring rulers. The Golden Bull of 1222 and Mongol invasion of 1241 reshaped political relationships and defenses.","institutions":[{"title":"Itinerant kingship","text":"Kings maintained their authority through personal visits, assemblies, and grants of rights; the early kingdom had no single fixed capital."},{"title":"Golden Bull of 1222","text":"Andrew II confirmed the rights of royal servants and other groups, providing an enduring basis for aristocratic politics in Hungary."}],"milestones":[{"year":1000,"title":"Stephen I crowned","text":"A Christian kingdom took shape around the turn of the millennium, supported by the monarchy and the Latin Church.","sourceIndexes":[0],"dateLabel":"1000/1001"},{"year":1222,"title":"Golden Bull issued","text":"Andrew II issued a charter adjusting relations between the crown, nobles, and royal servants.","sourceIndexes":[2]},{"year":1241,"title":"Mongol invasion","text":"The Hungarian army was defeated at Mohi, and Mongol forces advanced into the kingdom, causing widespread destruction.","sourceIndexes":[0]}],"sources":[{"title":"Hungary: A Short History","institution":"National Széchényi Library; C. A. Macartney","url":"https://mek.oszk.hu/02000/02086/02086.htm","supports":"The early kingdom and the events of 1222 and 1241"},{"title":"The King in the Saddle: The Árpád Dynasty and Itinerant Kingship","institution":"Hungarian Historical Review; researcher at the Slovak Academy of Sciences","url":"https://hunghist.org/archive/83-articles/808-2022-3-hudacek","supports":"The itinerant court, core territories, and practices of government"},{"title":"The Golden Bull of Hungary","institution":"Institute of History, ELTE Research Centre for the Humanities","url":"https://tti.abtk.hu/en/articles/institute/the-golden-bull-of-hungary","supports":"The reform context of the 1222 charter and its revision in 1231"}]},{"id":"polish-duchies","name":"Polish Duchies","aliases":["Kingdom of Poland","Duchy of Greater Poland","Duchy of Masovia","Duchy of Silesia","Grand Duchy of Kraków"],"matchPattern":"^(Kingdom of Poland|Duchy of Greater Poland|Duchy of Masovia|Duchy of Silesia|Grand Duchy of Kraków)$","matchYears":{"from":1138,"to":1319},"periodLabel":"The period of fragmentation · 1138–1320","center":"Kraków was the early seat of the senior duke and a symbol of royal authority. Poznań, Gniezno, Wrocław, Płock, and other cities had their own political centers.","overview":"After Bolesław III's death, branches of the Piast dynasty divided the lands, initially under an arrangement recognizing a senior duke. Succession struggles brought further fragmentation, while regional towns and church networks sustained shared connections. Coronations in 1295 and 1320 marked important stages in the restoration of kingship.","institutions":[{"title":"Dynastic partition","text":"Piast branches ruled separate duchies. Kraków's precedence was repeatedly contested, and central authority was not consistently effective."},{"title":"Restoring kingship","text":"Coronations did not immediately restore all former territories; regions were reunited at different times and in different ways."}],"milestones":[{"year":1138,"title":"Partition by inheritance","text":"After Bolesław III's death, his sons divided rule over Poland according to the succession settlement.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1241,"title":"Battle of Legnica","text":"Henry II died fighting the Mongols, frustrating attempts at unification led from Silesia.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1295,"title":"Przemysł II crowned","text":"The Polish royal title was restored, although the king's effective territory remained limited.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1320,"title":"Władysław I crowned","text":"His coronation in Kraków conventionally marks the end of the period of fragmentation.","sourceIndexes":[0,1]}],"sources":[{"title":"The period of fragmentation","institution":"Polish National Education Platform, ZPE","url":"https://zpe.gov.pl/a/przeczytaj/DagJKGgSf","supports":"The partition arrangements of 1138 and the conventional endpoint of 1320"},{"title":"Poland during the period of fragmentation","institution":"Polish National Education Platform, ZPE","url":"https://zpe.gov.pl/watek/LPA6ESLS6P/5/a/rozbicie-dzielnicowe/DFRPQ2MDB","supports":"The events of 1241, 1295, and 1320, and the chronology of fragmentation"},{"title":"Kraków","institution":"Oxford University Press","url":"https://academic.oup.com/reference/62384/reference-article-abstract/555068106?login=false","supports":"Kraków as the senior duke's seat and a political symbol"}]},{"id":"kievan-rus","name":"Kyivan Rus' and the Principalities of Rus'","aliases":["Kievan Rus'","Kievan Rus","Kyivan Rus'","Rus'","Principality of Kiev"],"matchPattern":"^(Kievan Rus['’]?|Kyivan Rus['’]?|Rus['’]|Principality of Kiev)$","periodLabel":"9th–13th centuries; multiple political centers emerged in the 12th","center":"Kyiv was an important princely and ecclesiastical center. Novgorod, Chernihiv, Smolensk, and other cities had their own princely and urban political life.","overview":"Rus' developed along river trade routes, shaped by Riurikid princes and local political forces. Kyiv long held a prominent position, while Christianization encouraged writing, architecture, and religious culture. Several principalities coexisted in the 12th century, and Mongol conquest later transformed their political relationships.","institutions":[{"title":"Dynastic networks","text":"Princes negotiated their rights through inheritance, alliances, warfare, and assemblies. Effective political power lay in several cities."},{"title":"Writing and faith","text":"Monasteries, churches, and manuscript production developed a distinctive culture in Rus', in continuing exchange with Byzantine and South Slavic traditions."}],"milestones":[{"year":988,"title":"Vladimir adopts Christianity","text":"This is the conventional date for the Kyivan ruler's adoption of Byzantine Christianity; the wider Christianization of society continued afterward.","sourceIndexes":[0],"dateLabel":"c. 988"},{"year":1097,"title":"Congress of princes at Liubech","text":"Princes discussed hereditary territories and cooperation against the Cumans, though disputes continued.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1240,"title":"Mongols capture Kyiv","text":"After attacks on other principalities of Rus', Mongol forces captured Kyiv, transforming the regional political order.","sourceIndexes":[0,2]}],"sources":[{"title":"The Golden Age of Kyivan Rus´","institution":"Harvard Ukrainian Research Institute, MAPA","url":"https://www.gis.huri.harvard.edu/golden-age-kyivan-rus","supports":"Christianization in 988, written culture, and the fall of Kyiv in 1240"},{"title":"Liubech congress of princes","institution":"Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, University of Alberta","url":"https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages/L/I/Liubechcongressofprinces.htm","supports":"The congress of 1097, the princes, and hereditary territories"},{"title":"Kievan Rus’ and Muscovy Under the Riurikids","institution":"Oxford University Press","url":"https://academic.oup.com/edited-volume/61799/chapter-abstract/546291388","supports":"Riurikid political networks and the Mongol conquest of 1237–1240"}]},{"id":"almohad-caliphate","name":"Almohad Caliphate","aliases":["Almohad Caliphate","Almohad Dynasty"],"matchPattern":"^(Almohad Caliphate|Almohad Dynasty)$","periodLabel":"Rose in the 12th century; rule at Marrakesh ended in 1269","center":"Marrakesh was the principal center of government; Seville was an important administrative and court center in al-Andalus.","overview":"The Almohad movement arose in the Maghreb, challenging the Almoravids through religious reform and political consolidation. After taking Marrakesh, it extended across much of North Africa and southern Iberia. Its courts supported philosophy, scholarship, and architecture, while wars and internal struggles gradually broke up the empire in the 13th century.","institutions":[{"title":"Religion and government","text":"The dynasty grew from Ibn Tumart's religious movement, closely linking religious reform to caliphal authority."},{"title":"Urban architecture","text":"The Kutubiyya Mosque and the minaret of Seville's great mosque exemplify Almohad urban building and its tradition of square towers."}],"milestones":[{"year":1147,"title":"Capture of Marrakesh","text":"Abd al-Mu'min captured the Almoravids' principal city, establishing the new dynasty's rule.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1195,"title":"Battle of Alarcos","text":"Almohad forces defeated Alfonso VIII's Castilian army.","sourceIndexes":[2]},{"year":1212,"title":"Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa","text":"The Almohads lost to a coalition of Iberian Christian kings, contributing to the peninsula's changing political balance.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1269,"title":"Loss of Marrakesh","text":"The Marinids captured Marrakesh, ending Almohad rule in Morocco.","sourceIndexes":[1]}],"sources":[{"title":"The Art of the Almoravid and Almohad Periods (ca. 1062–1269)","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/de/essays/the-art-of-the-almoravid-and-almohad-periods-ca-1062-1269","supports":"The dynasty, scholarship and architecture, and the battle of 1212"},{"title":"Almohad","institution":"Getty Research Institute, Art & Architecture Thesaurus","url":"https://www.getty.edu/vow/AATFullDisplay?find=300250085&logic=AND&note=&subjectid=300021528","supports":"Marrakesh in 1147 and the end of Almohad rule in 1269"},{"title":"UCLM and the Municipality of Poblete: the Battle of Alarcos","institution":"University of Castilla-La Mancha","url":"https://www.uclm.es/noticias/noticias2019/abril/ciudad-real/ruta_realidad_aumentada_alarcos","supports":"The battle of 1195 and the opposing forces"}]},{"id":"crown-of-aragon","name":"Crown of Aragon","aliases":["Crown of Aragon"],"matchPattern":"^Crown of Aragon$","periodLabel":"Dynastic union began to take shape in 1137","center":"The royal court traveled between its territories. Zaragoza, Barcelona, and later Valencia each served as important political centers.","overview":"The union of the Kingdom of Aragon and the house of the counts of Barcelona laid the foundations of the Crown of Aragon. Under a shared monarch, its territories retained their own laws, representative assemblies, and administrative traditions. Expansion into the Balearic Islands, Valencia, and the wider Mediterranean created a composite monarchy.","institutions":[{"title":"Shared monarch, distinct institutions","text":"Aragon, Catalonia, Valencia, and other territories maintained their own laws and assemblies while owing allegiance to a common monarch."},{"title":"Mediterranean networks","text":"Coastal trade, shipbuilding, and dynastic expansion connected eastern Iberia with the islands and southern Italy."}],"milestones":[{"year":1137,"title":"Aragon and Barcelona unite","text":"The betrothal of Petronilla and Ramon Berenguer IV, together with arrangements governing their rights, established the basis of the dynastic union.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1164,"title":"Petronilla transfers the kingdom","text":"The queen formally transferred her rights over Aragon to her son Alfonso II, who had already inherited the rights of the count of Barcelona.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1238,"title":"James I takes Valencia","text":"The Crown of Aragon expanded along Iberia's eastern coast, and Valencia developed its own institutions as a kingdom.","sourceIndexes":[2]}],"sources":[{"title":"Archives of the Crown of Aragon: Guide","institution":"Spanish Ministry of Culture, Archives of the Crown of Aragon","url":"https://www.cultura.gob.es/archivos-aca/en/dam/jcr%3A285526e5-3a98-4792-b61d-b4d0a6f6d6bb/guiaACAeng.pdf","supports":"The union of 1137, the Crown's territories, and archival organization"},{"title":"Petronilla's grant of the Kingdom of Aragon","institution":"Spanish Ministry of Culture, PARES archival catalog","url":"https://pares.cultura.gob.es/ParesBusquedas20/catalogo/description/12898662","supports":"The queen's 1164 document transferring the kingdom to Alfonso"},{"title":"Kings of Aragon","institution":"Government of Aragon","url":"https://www.aragon.es/estatuto-de-autonomia/reyes-de-aragon","supports":"Dynastic succession and the capture of Valencia in 1238"}]},{"id":"kingdom-of-castile","name":"Kingdom of Castile","aliases":["Kingdom of Castile","Kingdom of Castille","Crown of Castile"],"matchPattern":"^(Kingdom of Castil[l]?e|Crown of Castile)$","periodLabel":"Medieval Castile; permanent union with León from 1230","center":"The medieval court traveled between Burgos, Toledo, Valladolid, and other cities. After its conquest, Seville also became an important royal residence.","overview":"Castile developed from a northern Iberian frontier region into a kingdom, repeatedly joining with and separating from León. Ferdinand III held both crowns from 1230, establishing a lasting union. Southern conquests enlarged its territory and brought the complex task of integrating cities, diverse religious communities, and local laws.","institutions":[{"title":"Towns and local rights","text":"Municipal privileges, royal justice, and representative assemblies all shaped the kingdom's politics, while the court remained itinerant."},{"title":"Law and writing","text":"The Siete Partidas, compiled under Alfonso X, organized laws on kingship, justice, and social relations, profoundly influencing later legal traditions."}],"milestones":[{"year":1230,"title":"Castile and León united","text":"Ferdinand III inherited the throne of León, establishing a lasting union of the two kingdoms under one monarch.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1248,"title":"Capture of Seville","text":"Ferdinand III's army concluded the siege, extending the Crown of Castile's control over the Guadalquivir Valley.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1256,"title":"Compilation of the Siete Partidas","text":"Alfonso X supported jurists and scribes in compiling the legal text.","sourceIndexes":[1],"dateLabel":"1256–1265"}],"sources":[{"title":"The royal dynasties of Spain in the Middle Ages","institution":"Spanish Official State Gazette Agency, scholarly library","url":"https://www.boe.es/biblioteca_juridica/abrir_pdf.php?id=PUB-DH-2021-233","supports":"Ferdinand III's crowns in Castile and León and the events of 1230 and 1248"},{"title":"A fragment of Alfonso X's Partidas","institution":"Spanish Ministry of Culture, Historical Archive of the Nobility","url":"https://www.cultura.gob.es/en/cultura/areas/archivos/mc/archivos/nhn/destacados/documentos-destacados/partidas-alfonso-x.html","supports":"Royal patronage, purpose, and dating of the Siete Partidas"},{"title":"The itinerant court of Castile in the first half of the 15th century","institution":"Miguel de Cervantes Virtual Library; Francisco de Paula Cañas Gálvez","url":"https://www.cervantesvirtual.com/portales/la_celestina/obra/la-itinerancia-de-la-corte-de-castilla-durante-la-primera-mitad-del-siglo-xv-el-eje-burgos-toledo-escenario-burocratico-administrativo-y-politico-de-la-monarquia-en-tiempos-1223546/","supports":"The itinerant Castilian court and the Burgos–Toledo political axis"}]},{"id":"ottoman-empire","name":"Ottoman Empire","aliases":["Ottoman Empire"],"matchPattern":"^Ottoman Empire$","periodLabel":"c. 1300–1922","center":"Bursa and Edirne were early centers of government; Constantinople became the imperial capital after 1453.","overview":"The Ottomans grew from a frontier polity in northwestern Anatolia into an empire spanning Europe, Asia, and Africa. They gradually gained control over the Balkans and eastern Mediterranean, creating a new imperial center after taking Constantinople. The sultan's court and provincial cities supported administration, trade, and diverse artistic production.","institutions":[{"title":"Imperial court","text":"Officials, scholars, and artisans from many regions gathered at the sultan's court, making Constantinople a center of power and artistic patronage."},{"title":"Urban specialization","text":"İznik was renowned for ceramics and Bursa for silk textiles; cities beyond the capital also contributed to imperial cultural networks."}],"milestones":[{"year":1453,"title":"Capture of Constantinople","text":"Mehmed II took the Byzantine capital and developed it as the center of the Ottoman Empire.","sourceIndexes":[0,2]},{"year":1516,"title":"Conquest of the Mamluk heartlands","text":"Selim I defeated the Mamluk state, bringing Syria, Egypt, and other lands under Ottoman rule.","sourceIndexes":[0],"dateLabel":"1516–1517"},{"year":1922,"title":"Sultanate abolished","text":"The Ottoman sultanate ended, bringing the dynasty's temporal rule to a close.","sourceIndexes":[1]}],"sources":[{"title":"The Art of the Ottomans before 1600","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/the-art-of-the-ottomans-before-1600","supports":"Early expansion, the events of 1453 and 1517, and court and provincial artistic centers"},{"title":"The Greater Ottoman Empire, 1600–1800","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/ko/essays/the-greater-ottoman-empire-1600-1800","supports":"The empire's continental reach and the end of the sultanate in 1922"},{"title":"Balkan Peninsula, 1400–1600 A.D.","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/it/toah/ht/08/eusb.html","supports":"Edirne, the events of 1453, and Ottoman expansion in the Balkans"}]},{"id":"kingdom-scotland","name":"Kingdom of Scotland","aliases":["Kingdom of Scotland","Kingdom of Alba"],"matchPattern":"^(Kingdom of Scotland|Kingdom of Alba)$","periodLabel":"Medieval Scotland · 11th–14th centuries","center":"Scone was the traditional place of royal inauguration. The court traveled between Perth, Stirling, Dunfermline, and other royal residences.","overview":"Scotland gradually united several regions of northern Britain. In the 12th century, David I and his successors promoted monasteries, royal burghs, and coinage, extending royal authority through nobles and the Church. A succession crisis in the late 13th century led to prolonged war with England.","institutions":[{"title":"Royal burghs","text":"David I founded or supported royal burghs such as Stirling and Perth and issued Scottish silver coins, encouraging urban growth and trade."},{"title":"Monasteries and kingship","text":"Royal patronage supported monasteries such as Holyrood and Melrose. Religious institutions also managed estates and fostered education and written culture."}],"milestones":[{"year":1124,"title":"David I succeeds","text":"David succeeded his brother Alexander I; his reign encouraged the development of royal burghs, bishoprics, and monasteries.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1314,"title":"Battle of Bannockburn","text":"Robert I defeated Edward II's army, strengthening his control of Scotland, although the war continued.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1320,"title":"Declaration of Arbroath","text":"Scottish nobles wrote to the pope, asserting the kingdom's independence and the legitimacy of Robert I's rule.","sourceIndexes":[2]}],"sources":[{"title":"David I (r. 1124–1153)","institution":"The Royal Family, historical resources","url":"https://www.royal.uk/david-i-r-1124-1153","supports":"Accession in 1124, burghs, silver coinage, monasteries, and officials"},{"title":"Battle of Bannockburn: Inventory of Historic Battlefields","institution":"Historic Environment Scotland","url":"https://portal.historicenvironment.scot/apex/f?p=1505%3A300%3A%3A%3A%3A%3AVIEWTYPE%2CVIEWREF%3Adesignation%2CBTL4","supports":"The battle of 1314 and Robert I's political position"},{"title":"The Declaration of Arbroath","institution":"National Records of Scotland","url":"https://www.nrscotland.gov.uk/learning-and-events/the-declaration-of-arbroath/","supports":"The nature and political aims of the letter to the pope in 1320"}]},{"id":"kingdom-denmark","name":"Kingdom of Denmark","aliases":["Kingdom of Denmark"],"matchPattern":"^Kingdom of Denmark$","periodLabel":"The Valdemarian era · 1157–1241","center":"Kings traveled between castles and estates. Roskilde was a royal, minting, and episcopal center; Lund was the archbishop's seat.","overview":"Medieval Denmark included Jutland, the islands, and territories such as Scania in what is now southern Sweden. After 1157, the Valdemarian dynasty strengthened royal authority with bishops and nobles, expanding along the southern Baltic and into Estonia. Castles, dioceses, and towns supported the kingdom's government.","institutions":[{"title":"Itinerant kingship and castles","text":"Kings handled local affairs through royal journeys. Valdemar I built castles that served as both defenses and royal residences."},{"title":"Kingship and Church","text":"Bishop Absalon was a key ally of Valdemar I. Cathedrals, monasteries, and church estates formed networks of local power."}],"milestones":[{"year":1157,"title":"Valdemar I becomes sole king","text":"Following the Bloodfeast of Roskilde and the Battle of Grathe Heath, Valdemar defeated his rivals and ended the struggle for the throne.","sourceIndexes":[0,1]},{"year":1169,"title":"Conquest of Rügen","text":"Valdemar I and Absalon conquered Rügen. Arkona's religious center was destroyed, and Christianity was forcibly imposed on the island.","sourceIndexes":[0,1]},{"year":1219,"title":"Expedition to Estonia","text":"Valdemar II invaded Estonia, extending Danish power into its north as part of the Baltic crusades.","sourceIndexes":[1]}],"sources":[{"title":"Valdemar I the Great (1157–1182)","institution":"National Museum of Denmark","url":"https://natmus.dk/historisk-viden/temaer/kongeraekken/valdemar-1-den-store/","supports":"The events of 1157 and 1169, castles and royal travel, kingship, and Absalon"},{"title":"Denmark in the Middle Ages","institution":"Lex, Denmark's national encyclopedia / historian at Aarhus University","url":"https://lex.dk/Danmarks_historie_i_middelalderen","supports":"The kingdom's territory, the Valdemarian era, and the Estonian expedition of 1219"},{"title":"Denmark's Viking towns","institution":"Viking Ship Museum, Denmark","url":"https://www.vikingeskibsmuseet.dk/fagligt/e-laering/vikingetidens-geografi/spor-efter-vikingetiden-i-danmark/vikingetidens-byer","supports":"Roskilde's royal residence, mint, episcopal seat, and medieval urban importance"}]},{"id":"kingdom-norway","name":"Kingdom of Norway","aliases":["Kingdom of Norway","Old Kingdom of Norway"],"matchPattern":"^(Kingdom of Norway|Old Kingdom of Norway)$","periodLabel":"Medieval Norway · 12th–13th centuries","center":"The kings remained itinerant. Bergen was a major royal residence in the later 13th century, Oslo and Tønsberg also had palaces, and Nidaros was the archbishop's center.","overview":"Medieval Norway developed royal, diocesan, and port networks along its long coast. As the succession wars of the 12th and 13th centuries gradually ended, royal administration and law became more unified. Bergen's fish trade linked the North Sea kingdoms, while Norwegian rulers extended their influence to North Atlantic islands.","institutions":[{"title":"Local assemblies and written law","text":"Regional assemblies were central to legal traditions. The National Law of 1274 established shared rules on the foundations of existing regional laws."},{"title":"Ports and trade","text":"Ports such as Bergen gathered coastal produce. Dried fish and timber were major exports, while grain, malt, and cloth came from overseas."}],"milestones":[{"year":1217,"title":"Haakon IV acclaimed king","text":"Haakon Haakonsson was acclaimed king and gradually consolidated his rule through the ensuing power struggles.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1247,"title":"Coronation in Bergen","text":"A papal legate crowned Haakon IV at Bergen, strengthening the Norwegian monarchy's standing in Latin Christendom.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1274,"title":"National Law introduced","text":"Magnus VI promoted a common law code for the Norwegian kingdom, earning the name 'the Lawmender'.","sourceIndexes":[1]}],"sources":[{"title":"Haakon IV Haakonsson","institution":"Store norske leksikon, the Norwegian encyclopedia","url":"https://snl.no/H%C3%A5kon_4._H%C3%A5konsson","supports":"Accession in 1217 and coronation in Bergen in 1247"},{"title":"The National Law Anniversary 2024","institution":"National Library of Norway","url":"https://www.nb.no/en/national-law/","supports":"The National Law of 1274, regional assemblies, and legal traditions"},{"title":"Norway in the High Middle Ages","institution":"Store norske leksikon / historians at the University of South-Eastern Norway and NTNU","url":"https://snl.no/Norge_i_h%C3%B8ymiddelalderen","supports":"The itinerant court, major cities, maritime trade, and the growth of royal and church institutions"}]},{"id":"kingdom-sweden","name":"Kingdom of Sweden","aliases":["Kingdom of Sweden"],"matchPattern":"^Kingdom of Sweden$","periodLabel":"Formation of the medieval kingdom · 12th–13th centuries","center":"The royal household used several estates and castles. Uppsala was the archbishop's seat, the Lake Mälaren region became the political center, and Stockholm rose in the 13th century.","overview":"Sweden gradually formed around Svealand, Götaland, and neighboring regions. Rival dynasties contested the throne in the 12th century, while church organization and local laws strengthened regional connections. Under the House of Bjälbo in the 13th century, royal taxation, towns, and the knightly aristocracy developed further.","institutions":[{"title":"Provinces and law","text":"Each province retained its own laws and assembly traditions. Local customary laws were written down during the 13th century; a kingdom-wide code came later."},{"title":"Mounted service and tax exemption","text":"The Ordinance of Alsnö, issued around 1280, linked mounted military service to the king with tax exemption, encouraging the formation of a secular nobility."}],"milestones":[{"year":1164,"title":"Archbishopric of Uppsala","text":"Uppsala became an archiepiscopal seat, giving Sweden its own ecclesiastical province.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1250,"title":"Valdemar succeeds","text":"The House of Erik ended, and Valdemar Birgersson became king, with his father Birger Jarl playing the leading political role.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1280,"title":"Ordinance of Alsnö","text":"An ordinance under Magnus established tax exemptions for those providing mounted service and restricted abuses such as forcibly demanding food and lodging.","sourceIndexes":[1,2],"dateLabel":"c. 1280"}],"sources":[{"title":"When Sweden became Sweden","institution":"Swedish History Museum","url":"https://historiska.se/en/explore-history/history-hub/when-sweden-became-sweden/","supports":"Unification, the ecclesiastical province of 1164, succession in 1250, provincial written laws, and political centers"},{"title":"Ordinance of Alsnö","institution":"Nationalencyklopedin, the Swedish national encyclopedia","url":"https://www.ne.se/uppslagsverk/encyklopedi/article/alsn%C3%B6-stadga","supports":"The ordinance's provisions and the dating of its compilation to around 1280 despite dates in surviving copies"},{"title":"Magnus Birgersson","institution":"National Archives of Sweden, Dictionary of Swedish National Biography","url":"https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Presentation.aspx?forceOrdinarySite=true&id=10148","supports":"Ordinances under Magnus, royal power, and aristocratic military service"}]},{"id":"kingdom-portugal","name":"Kingdom of Portugal","aliases":["Kingdom of Portugal"],"matchPattern":"^Kingdom of Portugal$","periodLabel":"The kingdom's formation and expansion · 12th–13th centuries","center":"Coimbra was an important residence and political center for the early kings. The court traveled, while Lisbon and Santarém grew in importance as the kingdom expanded south.","overview":"Portugal developed from a county on the edge of León into an independent kingdom. Afonso I and his successors expanded southward, consolidating rule through papal recognition, town charters, and monastic networks. Wars and counteroffensives involving Muslim powers repeatedly shifted the frontier as the kingdom advanced toward the Algarve.","institutions":[{"title":"Town charters","text":"Royal charters known as forais defined townspeople's rights and obligations. Frontier towns used them to attract settlers and support defense."},{"title":"Church and court","text":"Coimbra's religious institutions supplied clerics skilled in documentation to the royal court. Monasteries received lands and patronage and contributed to dynastic memory and local life."}],"milestones":[{"year":1143,"title":"Meeting at Zamora","text":"Alfonso VII recognized Afonso Henriques's royal status; Portugal's monarchy and external relations continued to develop.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1147,"title":"Capture of Lisbon","text":"With help from northern European crusaders, Afonso I took Lisbon, extending royal control to the lower Tagus.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1179,"title":"Papal recognition of kingship","text":"Alexander III issued the bull Manifestis Probatum, confirming the royal status of Afonso I and his successors.","sourceIndexes":[0,2]}],"sources":[{"title":"History of Portugal: Medieval Portugal","institution":"NOVA University Lisbon research repository; Diogo Ferreira and Paulo Dias","url":"https://research.unl.pt/ws/portalfiles/portal/3381604/CHAM_Paulo_Dias_Fiogo_Ferreira_embargo_18_meses.pdf","supports":"Pages 25–28 and 52–53: the events of 1143, 1147, and 1179, Coimbra, charters, and southern expansion"},{"title":"Death Commemoration Strategies in Medieval Portugal: The Case of Coimbra","institution":"Religions, scholarly journal","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2077-1444/14/12/1443","supports":"Coimbra as a royal and ecclesiastical center, clerics at court, and the growth of Tagus towns"},{"title":"The bull Manifestis Probatum","institution":"German Historical Museum","url":"https://www.dhm.de/archiv/ausstellungen/neue-welten/pt/popups/popup_0006.html","supports":"The papal bull of 1179 and recognition of Portuguese kingship"}]},{"id":"kingdom-navarre","name":"Kingdom of Navarre","aliases":["Kingdom of Navarre","Kingdom of Navarra"],"matchPattern":"^(Kingdom of Navarre|Kingdom of Navarra)$","periodLabel":"The Kingdom of Pamplona–Navarre · 12th–13th centuries","center":"Pamplona was the traditional political and ecclesiastical center. Kings also used residences such as Tudela, where Sancho VII spent long periods.","overview":"Navarre grew from a kingdom centered on Pamplona, between the Pyrenean passes and the Ebro Valley. After separating from Aragon in 1134, its kings maintained their rule between Castile and Aragon. The House of Champagne inherited the throne in 1234, while local chartered laws retained their importance.","institutions":[{"title":"Local laws and privileges","text":"Towns and communities followed their own fueros. Estella, Pamplona, Tudela, and other towns developed interacting legal traditions."},{"title":"Succession and institutions","text":"Under the House of Champagne, Navarre retained its own laws and institutions; the monarch's other possessions did not become part of a single administrative territory."}],"milestones":[{"year":1134,"title":"Separation from Aragon","text":"After Alfonso I's death, García Ramírez was acclaimed king, ending the shared monarchy that had existed since 1076.","sourceIndexes":[0,1]},{"year":1162,"title":"The title King of Navarre adopted","text":"Sancho VI replaced the traditional title 'King of the Pamplonese' with 'King of Navarre', changing the way royal authority was expressed.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1234,"title":"The House of Champagne succeeds","text":"After Sancho VII's death, Theobald I, count of Champagne, inherited the Navarrese throne.","sourceIndexes":[1]}],"sources":[{"title":"Navarrese law in its foundational texts","institution":"University of Navarre","url":"https://www.unav.edu/web/catedra-patrimonio/aula-abierta/exposiciones/el-derecho-navarro","supports":"The shared monarchy of 1076–1134, the royal title of 1162, and local fueros and legal traditions"},{"title":"Navarre's foral system: The Kingdom of Navarre","institution":"Government of Navarre / Institute of Public Administration","url":"https://www.navarra.es/documents/48192/6735340/3.%20El%20Regimen%20Foral%20de%20Navarra.pdf/aaf86928-00f4-b35e-d816-0e3ea5e1d626","supports":"Separation in 1134, the Champagne succession in 1234, and distinct institutions"},{"title":"Municipal Archive of Tudela: Institutional history","institution":"Spanish Ministry of Culture, census of archives","url":"https://censoarchivos.cultura.gob.es/CensoGuia/archivodetail.htm?id=55975","supports":"Tudela as a royal residence and place of document issuance under Sancho VII"}]},{"id":"second-bulgarian-empire","name":"Second Bulgarian Empire","aliases":["Second Bulgarian Empire"],"matchPattern":"^Second Bulgarian Empire$","periodLabel":"From 1185; the rise and expansion of the Asen dynasty","center":"Tarnovo, now Veliko Tarnovo, was the dynasty's political and religious center, with its court and fortresses on hills encircled by the Yantra River.","overview":"The brothers Peter and Asen rebelled against Byzantine rule in 1185, restoring Bulgarian kingship. Their successors Kaloyan and Ivan Asen II expanded in the Balkans, fighting the Latin Empire and Byzantine successor states. Tarnovo's churches, royal tombs, and inscriptions preserve the memory of this era.","institutions":[{"title":"Royal religious patronage","text":"The dynasty supported churches and monasteries in Tarnovo. The Church of the Holy Forty Martyrs commemorated victory and served for royal burial and the veneration of relics."},{"title":"Inscriptions and memory","text":"The Church of the Holy Forty Martyrs preserves inscribed columns of earlier Bulgarian rulers and Ivan Asen II, gathering royal memorials from different periods in one place."}],"milestones":[{"year":1185,"title":"Peter and Asen rebel","text":"The brothers led an uprising against Byzantium, beginning the restoration of the Bulgarian kingdom.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1205,"title":"Battle of Adrianople","text":"Kaloyan defeated the Latin Empire's army and captured Emperor Baldwin I.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1230,"title":"Battle of Klokotnitsa","text":"Ivan Asen II defeated the forces of Epirus under Theodore Komnenos, greatly expanding his influence in the Balkans.","sourceIndexes":[2]}],"sources":[{"title":"Burgas Region during the Middle Ages","institution":"Regional Historical Museum Burgas","url":"https://www.burgasmuseums.bg/en/encdetail/burgas-region-during-middle-ages-184","supports":"The uprising of 1185, the restoration of Bulgarian kingship, and territorial changes in the Balkans"},{"title":"Glorious rulers of the Second Bulgarian Kingdom come to life in new sculpture museum","institution":"Bulgarian National Radio / report on the Tarnovo museum","url":"https://old-news.bnr.bg/en/post/100184196/glorious-rulers-of-the-second-bulgarian-kingdom-come-to-life-in-new-sculpture-museum","supports":"The court at Tarnovo and Kaloyan's capture of Baldwin in 1205"},{"title":"Church of the Holy Forty Martyrs and the Great Lavra Monastic Complex","institution":"Regional Museum of History, Veliko Tarnovo","url":"https://www.museumvt.com/en/catalog/holy-forty-martyr-s-church-and-the-great-laurel-monastic-complex","supports":"The victory of 1230, royal church, tombs, and inscribed columns"}]},{"id":"kingdom-serbia","matchYears":{"from":1000,"to":1400},"name":"Principality and Kingdom of Serbia","aliases":["Grand Principality of Serbia","Kingdom of Serbia"],"matchPattern":"^(Grand Principality of Serbia|Kingdom of Serbia)$","periodLabel":"The Nemanjić dynasty · 12th–13th centuries; royal crown acquired in 1217","center":"Ras and the Raška region formed the dynasty's early heartland. The court used several residences; Žiča became an important ecclesiastical and coronation center in the 13th century.","overview":"The Nemanjić dynasty consolidated Serbian rule around Raška in the later 12th century. Stefan received a royal crown in 1217, and Sava subsequently established an independent church organization. Royal monasteries combined Byzantine painting with western architectural elements, sustaining dynastic patronage and cultural traditions.","institutions":[{"title":"Kingship and an independent Church","text":"Sava became archbishop in 1219. The Serbian Church gained autocephaly and developed a network of bishoprics serving the kingdom."},{"title":"Royal monasteries","text":"Studenica, founded by Nemanja, served both religious life and dynastic commemoration. Its marble church and frescoes reflect artistic exchange between east and west."}],"milestones":[{"year":1196,"title":"Nemanja abdicates","text":"Stefan Nemanja abdicated in favor of his son Stefan, then became a monk under the name Simeon.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1217,"title":"Stefan receives a royal crown","text":"Stefan obtained a crown through the pope, raising the principality to the status of a kingdom.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1219,"title":"Sava becomes archbishop","text":"At Nicaea, Sava secured recognition of the Church's autocephaly and became Serbia's first archbishop.","sourceIndexes":[1]}],"sources":[{"title":"Studenica Monastery: The monastery","institution":"Studenica Monastery","url":"https://manastirstudenica.rs/en/monastery/","supports":"Construction in 1186–1196 and Nemanja's abdication and entry into monastic life in 1196"},{"title":"History of Serbian Diplomacy and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs","institution":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Serbia","url":"https://www.mfa.rs/lat/ministarstvo/istorijat","supports":"The royal crown of 1217, Žiča, and Sava and Church autocephaly in 1219"},{"title":"Studenica Monastery","institution":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/389/","supports":"Royal monasteries, architecture and frescoes, and Sava's church organization"}]},{"id":"sultanate-rum","name":"Sultanate of Rum","aliases":["Sultanate of Rum","Sultanate of Rûm","Seljuk Sultanate of Rum","Sultanate of Iconium"],"matchPattern":"^(Sultanate of Rum|Sultanate of Rûm|Seljuk Sultanate of Rum|Sultanate of Iconium)$","periodLabel":"The Anatolian Seljuq state · Late 11th–early 14th centuries","center":"Initially based at Nicaea, the government moved inland after losing the city. Konya became its principal center; Kayseri and Sivas were also important cities.","overview":"A branch of the Seljuq dynasty established the Sultanate of Rum in Anatolia, with Konya as its principal center. Court and urban culture drew on Persian, Islamic, and local Christian traditions, while trade routes, caravanserais, and religious buildings flourished. After defeat by the Mongols in 1243, the sultanate became their vassal.","institutions":[{"title":"Trade routes and caravanserais","text":"Sultans and other patrons built large caravanserais along trade routes, providing accommodation for merchants and their goods and promoting exchange between inland cities."},{"title":"Artistic traditions","text":"Mosques, madrasas, palaces, and tile decoration combined Perso-Islamic traditions and Turkic elements with local Byzantine and Armenian craftsmanship."}],"milestones":[{"year":1097,"title":"Loss of Nicaea","text":"During the First Crusade, Nicaea passed out of Rum's control, shifting the state's center toward inland Anatolia.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1176,"title":"Battle of Myriokephalon","text":"Kilij Arslan II defeated Manuel I's Byzantine army, halting its advance toward Konya.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1243,"title":"Battle of Köse Dağ","text":"The army of Rum was defeated by the Mongols; the sultanate subsequently paid tribute and came under Mongol suzerainty.","sourceIndexes":[0,2]}],"sources":[{"title":"Anatolia and the Caucasus, 1000–1400 A.D.","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/ht/07/waa.html","supports":"The loss of Nicaea in 1097, Konya, 13th-century trade and buildings, and defeat and tribute in 1243"},{"title":"Battle of Myriokephalon","institution":"Turkish Diyanet Foundation, Encyclopaedia of Islam","url":"https://islamansiklopedisi.org.tr/miryokefalon-savasi","supports":"The opposing rulers and Byzantine defeat in 1176"},{"title":"The Art of the Seljuq Period in Anatolia (1081–1307)","institution":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/the-art-of-the-seljuq-period-in-anatolia-1081-1307","supports":"The Seljuq branch, Mongol suzerainty, caravanserais, and artistic traditions"}]},{"id":"kingdom-georgia","name":"Kingdom of Georgia","aliases":["Kingdom of Georgia"],"matchPattern":"^Kingdom of Georgia$","periodLabel":"Georgia's flourishing kingdom · 12th–early 13th centuries","center":"Kutaisi was an important early center. After David IV captured Tbilisi in 1122, it became the kingdom's principal political center.","overview":"Medieval Georgia was an important Christian kingdom south of the Caucasus. David IV consolidated royal authority, and the kingdom flourished under Tamar. Royal patronage supported monasteries, scholarship, and art. Scholars at Gelati translated Greek works, while urban and church networks linked the Caucasus with the Byzantine world.","institutions":[{"title":"Monasteries and scholarship","text":"Gelati Monastery was a center of scholarship and education. Scholars translated Greek philosophy and theology, while scribes copied and illuminated manuscripts."},{"title":"Tamar and royal succession","text":"Tamar was crowned co-ruler and heir during her father's lifetime. She established her authority as a ruling queen within a political world shaped by clergy and nobles."}],"milestones":[{"year":1106,"title":"Gelati Monastery begun","text":"David IV began building Gelati near Kutaisi; royal patronage made it a religious and scholarly center.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1122,"title":"Capture of Tbilisi","text":"David IV captured Tbilisi, which became the kingdom's principal political center.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1184,"title":"Tamar begins sole rule","text":"Already a co-ruler, Tamar ruled in her own right after her father's death. Her reign became a major period of medieval Georgian prosperity.","sourceIndexes":[0,2]}],"sources":[{"title":"Gelati Monastery","institution":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/710","supports":"Construction from 1106, Kutaisi, royal patronage, scholarly translation, and Tamar's era"},{"title":"Tbilisi: Getty Thesaurus of Geographic Names","institution":"Getty Research Institute","url":"https://www.getty.edu/vow/TGNFullDisplay?english=Y&find=famagusta&nation=&place=&subjectid=7012268","supports":"David IV's capture of the city in 1122 and its role as the kingdom's political center"},{"title":"Tamar of Georgia (1184–1213) and the Language of Female Power","institution":"Arc Humanities Press / Cambridge Core; Lois Huneycutt","url":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/companion-to-global-queenship/tamar-of-georgia-1184-1213-and-the-language-of-female-power/5FFB2BFF762663E5BCFE60E418C07CA7","supports":"Tamar's shared and sole rule, female royal power, and relations with nobles and clergy"}]},{"id":"iceland-history","aliases":["Icelandic Commonwealth","Iceland — Old Covenant transition","Iceland under the Norwegian crown","Iceland under the Danish–Norwegian crown","Iceland under the Danish crown","Kingdom of Iceland","Republic of Iceland","Iceland"],"matchPattern":"^(Icelandic Commonwealth|Iceland — Old Covenant transition|Iceland under the Norwegian crown|Iceland under the Danish–Norwegian crown|Iceland under the Danish crown|Kingdom of Iceland|Republic of Iceland|Iceland)$","name":"Iceland · Commonwealth to Republic","periodLabel":"From 930; submission to Norway in 1262–1264, sovereignty in 1918, republic in 1944","center":"Þingvellir was the early assembly site; modern national institutions are based in Reykjavík.","overview":"Iceland moved from an assembly-based Commonwealth into Norwegian and later Danish–Norwegian royal rule. Constitutional reform and home rule preceded sovereignty and the republic. Wartime foreign forces did not become the island’s sovereign authority.","institutions":[{"title":"Assembly and law","text":"The Commonwealth’s Law Council legislated and dealt with legal disputes. Under Norwegian rule, parliament shared legislative responsibilities with the king."},{"title":"Sovereignty and government","text":"Iceland became a sovereign kingdom in 1918 and ended the personal union with Denmark when it became a republic in 1944."}],"milestones":[{"year":930,"date":"930","title":"Alþing established","text":"The assembly at Þingvellir became the central institution of the Icelandic Commonwealth.","sourceIndexes":[0]},{"year":1262,"date":"1262–1264","title":"The Old Covenant","text":"Submission to the Norwegian king took place over several years, ending the Commonwealth.","sourceIndexes":[1]},{"year":1380,"date":"1380","title":"Danish–Norwegian union","text":"Denmark and Norway came under a shared monarch; Iceland followed the Norwegian crown into this monarchy.","sourceIndexes":[1,2]},{"year":1814,"date":"January 1814","title":"Iceland after the Treaty of Kiel","text":"Iceland, Greenland and the Faroes remained with Denmark when Norway was ceded to Sweden.","sourceIndexes":[3]},{"year":1874,"date":"1874","title":"A constitution for Iceland","text":"A constitution for Icelandic affairs became a step toward greater self-government.","sourceIndexes":[4]},{"year":1904,"date":"1904","title":"Home rule takes effect","text":"The minister responsible for Iceland moved to Reykjavík and became answerable to Alþingi.","sourceIndexes":[4]},{"year":1918,"date":"1 December 1918","title":"A sovereign kingdom","text":"The Union Act made Iceland a sovereign state sharing a monarch with Denmark.","sourceIndexes":[4]},{"year":1940,"date":"10 May 1940","title":"British occupation","text":"British forces occupied strategic sites. Their presence did not transfer Icelandic sovereignty to Britain.","sourceIndexes":[7,8]},{"year":1941,"date":"1 July 1941","title":"Defence agreement with the United States","text":"The agreement recognized Icelandic sovereignty as American forces began supplementing and replacing British forces.","sourceIndexes":[7,8]},{"year":1944,"date":"17 June 1944","title":"Republic established","text":"The republican constitution entered into force at Þingvellir; Sveinn Björnsson was elected president.","sourceIndexes":[5,6]}],"sources":[{"title":"History","institution":"Þingvellir National Park","url":"https://www.thingvellir.is/en/education/history/","supports":"Alþing established around 930; Commonwealth period ending with submission beginning in 1262."},{"title":"Althingi: parliamentary information booklet","institution":"Alþingi","url":"https://www.althingi.is/pdf/enska.pdf","supports":"Old Covenant process 1262–1264; later Danish monarchy; parliamentary institutions; sovereignty on 1 December 1918; republic on 17 June 1944."},{"title":"Velkommen statsborger i Norge — Historien","institution":"Government of Norway","url":"https://www.regjeringen.no/no/dokumenter/velkommen-statsborger-i-norge-ny-gavebok-til-utdeling-pa-statsborgerseremonier/id3089381/?ch=3","supports":"Danish–Norwegian union dated 1380–1814; institutional change after 1536–1537. The overlay names the shared crown broadly, without implying unchanged constitutional arrangements."},{"title":"Nú er tíð að vakna: Kristján Friðrik og sjálfstæðisbarátta Noregs og Íslands","institution":"Iceland Ministry for Foreign Affairs","url":"https://www.stjornarradid.is/frettir/grein/2014-05-27-Nu-er-tid-ad-vakna-Kristjan-Fridrik-og-sjalfstaedisbaratta-Noregs-og-Islands/","supports":"January 1814 Treaty of Kiel: Iceland, Greenland and the Faroes remained connected to Denmark when Norway was ceded."},{"title":"Centennial of the Danish–Icelandic Union Act of 1918","institution":"Law Library of Congress","url":"https://blogs.loc.gov/law/2018/11/centennial-of-the-danish-icelandic-union-act-of-1918/","supports":"1874 constitution, home rule effective in 1904, sovereign personal union effective 1 December 1918."},{"title":"Picturing a Nation: Capturing the Founding of the Republic in 1944","institution":"National Museum of Iceland","url":"https://www.thjodminjasafn.is/en/syningar/thjod-i-mynd-myndefni-fra-stofnun-lydveldis-1944","supports":"Republic founded 17 June 1944."},{"title":"The Icelandic Minister (Thors) to the Secretary of State, 17 June 1944","institution":"Icelandic Government; published in US Department of State, FRUS 1944, vol. III, document 915","url":"https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d915","supports":"Republican constitution entered into force on 17 June 1944; election of Sveinn Björnsson."},{"title":"The Department of State to the British Embassy","institution":"US Department of State, FRUS 1944, vol. III, document 907","url":"https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d907","supports":"Independent state since 1918; the 1941 defence agreement explicitly respected Icelandic independence and sovereignty."},{"title":"The Icelandic Ministry for Foreign Affairs to the American Legation in Iceland","institution":"Iceland Ministry for Foreign Affairs; published in US Department of State, FRUS 1943, vol. II, document 277","url":"https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v02/d277","supports":"British occupation began 10 May 1940; 1941 defence arrangements provided for American forces to supplement and replace them. Military presence is distinct from sovereignty."}]}]};
